$EXTERN Prout, Chr, Ord, Add, Sub, Compare, Upper, Symb;

* Refal-authored lexer + parser + checker + emitter for Classic Refal-5.
*
* The lexer is the same tokeniser as examples/lexer.ref, but it emits token
* TERMS rather than display text:
*   (Ident W)  (Num D)  (Str C)  (Var s X)
*   LP RP LB RB LA RA EQ SEMI COMMA COLON EXTERN ENTRY
* Refal-5 has no module system, so the two stages live in one file here rather
* than the parser importing the lexer. They will be merged into compiler.ref
* alongside the checker and emitter.
*
* The AST is printed by Prout, which renders structural brackets as
* parentheses:
*   (PROG item...)
*   (EXT name...)                        an $EXTERN declaration
*   (FUN (Ident Name) (VIS ENTRY) s...)  a function; VIS is ENTRY or LOCAL
*   (SENT (pattern...) (cond...) (result...))
*   (COND (expr...) (pattern...))
*   (SYM c) (ID Name) (NUM d) (Var s X) (BR term...) (CALL (ID N) term...)
*
* Grammar covered: $EXTERN, $ENTRY and local functions, multi-sentence
* functions, patterns, conditions (, expr : pattern), results, calls and
* structural brackets, blocks as sentence endings and in condition position,
* one-character variable shorthand, /* */ block comments and reals. Every
* example in examples/ that the Rust bootstrap will lower comes back
* byte-identical from this file -- see the corpus sweep test.

* The CLI runs Go, so Go is the compiler: it emits. Passing CHECK as a first
* argument asks for the checker's verdict instead, which is what the checker
* tests drive, GRAPH asks for the section 4.2 seed graph, RESIDUALIZE asks for
* the residual program the cleaned graph denotes, and DRIVE contracts the closed
* entry configuration.
*
* The mode that is *not* recognised gets its own sentence, and it is load
* bearing. A mode-shaped call -- two brackets -- that names nothing this file
* implements would otherwise reach the compile sentence below, which would try
* to parse `('MODE') (source)` as a program and spin. Reporting is the point: a
* bug in a stage must not look like a hang.
*
* A duplicate sentence printing a marker is NOT the way to do that, and this
* file used to carry three of them. Refal-5 does not fall through to the next
* sentence when a right-hand side fails -- `evaluate_sentences` returns the
* result of the selected sentence directly, and a probe confirms it: `Mid { =
* <Boom>; = 'caught'; }` reports `no sentence matched in function Boom` rather
* than reaching the second sentence. So a duplicate of the sentence above it is
* unreachable, and a stage failure is already loud. What is reachable is the
* case below: an argument shape no mode claims.
* Go's own pattern is a single bare expression variable, and that is deliberate:
* it is the shape the supercompiler will partition. A driver can only branch on
* an argument it does not yet know, and `split_configuration` refuses to
* partition a configuration whose pattern is anything more specific. With the
* dispatch behind `Go` instead of inside it, `refal residualize-driven` can drive
* the compiler: the mode is an unknown expression, every mode sentence becomes a
* branch, and the branches are folded back into a generated decision tree. The
* mode table lives in `Dispatch` and is otherwise unchanged, so the CLI contract
* -- one bracket for a compile, two for a named mode -- is exactly what it was.
$ENTRY Go {
  e.Args = <Dispatch e.Args>;
}

Dispatch {
  ('CHECK') (e.Source) =
    <Prout <Check <Parse <Lex (<Chr 10>) <StripCR (<Chr 13>) e.Source>>>>>;
  ('GRAPH') (e.Source) =
    <Prout <Graph <Parse <Lex (<Chr 10>) <StripCR (<Chr 13>) e.Source>>>>>;
  ('RESIDUALIZE') (e.Source) =
    <Prout <Residualize <Parse <Lex (<Chr 10>) <StripCR (<Chr 13>) e.Source>>>>>;
  ('DRIVE') (e.Source) =
    <Prout <Drive <Parse <Lex (<Chr 10>) <StripCR (<Chr 13>) e.Source>>>>>;
  ('DRIVE-SYMBOLIC') (e.Source) =
    <Prout <DriveSym (SYM) e.Source>>;
  ('DRIVE-SYMBOLIC-CONFIGURATIONS') (e.Source) =
    <Prout <DriveSym (SYMCFG) e.Source>>;
  ('DRIVE-SYMBOLIC-NEIGHBORHOODS') (e.Source) =
    <Prout <DriveSym (SYMNEI) e.Source>>;
  ('DRIVE-SYMBOLIC-BOTH') (e.Source) =
    <Prout <DriveSym (SYMBOTH) e.Source>>;
  ('DRIVE-SYMBOLIC-INTERPRETIVE') (e.Source) =
    <Prout <DriveSym (SYMI) e.Source>>;
  ('RESIDUALIZE-DRIVEN') (e.Source) =
    <Prout <DsRdRun e.Source>>;
  (e.Mode) (e.Source) = <Prout 'unknown mode: ' e.Mode>;
  (e.Source) = <Compile <Lex (<Chr 10>) <StripCR (<Chr 13>) e.Source>>>;
}

* Check first, and emit only what checks.
Compile {
  e.Tokens = <OnChecked <Check <Parse e.Tokens>> <Parse e.Tokens>>;
}

OnChecked {
  "OK" (PROG e.Items) = <Prout <Emit (PROG e.Items)>>;
  e.Msg (PROG e.Items) = <Prout e.Msg>;
}

* ---------------------------------------------------------------- lexer

* Carriage returns are not significant, but CRLF sources would otherwise lex
* a bare \r as an identifier.
StripCR {
  (e.CR) = ;
  (e.CR) e.CR e.R = <StripCR (e.CR) e.R>;
  (e.CR) s.C e.R = s.C <StripCR (e.CR) e.R>;
}

Lex {
  (e.NL) = ;
  (e.NL) " " e.R = <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) e.NL e.R = <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) '*' e.R = <SkipLine (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) '(' e.R = LP <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) ')' e.R = RP <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) '{' e.R = LB <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) '}' e.R = RB <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) '<' e.R = LA <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) '>' e.R = RA <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) '=' e.R = EQ <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) ';' e.R = SEMI <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) ',' e.R = COMMA <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) ':' e.R = COLON <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) '$EXTERNAL' e.R = EXTERN <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) '$EXTRN' e.R = EXTERN <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) '$ENTRY' e.R = ENTRY <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) '$EXTERN' e.R = EXTERN <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) 's.' e.R = <VarEmit (e.NL) ('s') <TakeWord (e.NL) () e.R>>;
  (e.NL) 't.' e.R = <VarEmit (e.NL) ('t') <TakeWord (e.NL) () e.R>>;
  (e.NL) 'e.' e.R = <VarEmit (e.NL) ('e') <TakeWord (e.NL) () e.R>>;
  (e.NL) "'" e.R = <StrTok (e.NL) ("'") () e.R>;
  (e.NL) '"' e.R = <StrTok (e.NL) ('"') () e.R>;
* One-character variable shorthand: s1, tA, eX. Identifiers cannot begin with a
* lower-case letter, so a leading s/t/e is always a variable. Juxtaposition
* falls out: s1s2s3 is three variables, because the next 's' starts a new one.
  (e.NL) 's' s.I e.R = (Var 's' s.I) <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) 't' s.I e.R = (Var 't' s.I) <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) 'e' s.I e.R = (Var 'e' s.I) <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) '/' '*' e.R = <SkipBlock (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) s.C e.R = <WordTok (e.NL) s.C e.R>;
}

* A block comment runs to the closing */, or to the end of the source.
SkipBlock {
  (e.NL) = ;
  (e.NL) '*' '/' e.R = <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) s.C e.R = <SkipBlock (e.NL) e.R>;
}

SkipLine {
  (e.NL) = ;
  (e.NL) e.NL e.R = <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) s.C e.R = <SkipLine (e.NL) e.R>;
}

TakeWord {
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) = (e.Acc);
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) " " e.R = (e.Acc) " " e.R;
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) e.NL e.R = (e.Acc) e.NL e.R;
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) '(' e.R = (e.Acc) '(' e.R;
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) ')' e.R = (e.Acc) ')' e.R;
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) '{' e.R = (e.Acc) '{' e.R;
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) '}' e.R = (e.Acc) '}' e.R;
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) '<' e.R = (e.Acc) '<' e.R;
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) '>' e.R = (e.Acc) '>' e.R;
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) '=' e.R = (e.Acc) '=' e.R;
* '.' is not a terminator: a real such as 12.5 or +4E2 is one token. The s./t./e.
* variable rules above have already taken the dot that separates kind from index.
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) ';' e.R = (e.Acc) ';' e.R;
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) ',' e.R = (e.Acc) ',' e.R;
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) ':' e.R = (e.Acc) ':' e.R;
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) "'" e.R = (e.Acc) "'" e.R;
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) '"' e.R = (e.Acc) '"' e.R;
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) '$' e.R = (e.Acc) '$' e.R;
  (e.NL) (e.Acc) s.C e.R = <TakeWord (e.NL) (e.Acc s.C) e.R>;
}

WordTok {
  (e.NL) s.C e.R, <IsDigit s.C> : '1' =
    <NumEmit (e.NL) <TakeWord (e.NL) (s.C) e.R>>;
  (e.NL) s.C e.R =
    <IdentEmit (e.NL) <TakeWord (e.NL) (s.C) e.R>>;
}

IsDigit {
  '0' = '1';
  '1' = '1';
  '2' = '1';
  '3' = '1';
  '4' = '1';
  '5' = '1';
  '6' = '1';
  '7' = '1';
  '8' = '1';
  '9' = '1';
  s.C = ;
}

VarEmit {
  (e.NL) (e.K) (e.W) e.Rest = (Var e.K e.W) <Lex (e.NL) e.Rest>;
}

NumEmit {
  (e.NL) (e.W) e.Rest = (Num e.W) <Lex (e.NL) e.Rest>;
}

IdentEmit {
  (e.NL) (e.W) e.Rest = (Ident e.W) <Lex (e.NL) e.Rest>;
}

StrTok {
  (e.NL) (e.Q) (e.Acc) = (Str e.Acc);
  (e.NL) (e.Q) (e.Acc) e.Q e.Q e.R = <StrTok (e.NL) (e.Q) (e.Acc e.Q) e.R>;
  (e.NL) (e.Q) (e.Acc) e.Q e.R = (Str e.Acc) <Lex (e.NL) e.R>;
  (e.NL) (e.Q) (e.Acc) s.C e.R = <StrTok (e.NL) (e.Q) (e.Acc s.C) e.R>;
}

* --------------------------------------------------------------- parser

Parse {
  e.Tokens = (PROG <Items e.Tokens>);
}

Items {
  = ;
* A semicolon may separate top-level definitions.
  SEMI e.Rest = <Items e.Rest>;
  EXTERN e.Rest = <JoinItem <OneExtern e.Rest>>;
  ENTRY e.Rest = <JoinItem <OneFun (VIS ENTRY) e.Rest>>;
  (Ident e.N) e.Rest = <JoinItem <OneFun (VIS LOCAL) (Ident e.N) e.Rest>>;
}

* e.Item holds the *contents* of the item bracket, so it must be re-wrapped.
* Emitting it flat drops the brackets and flattens the whole AST.
JoinItem {
  (e.Item) e.Rest = (e.Item) <Items e.Rest>;
}

OneExtern {
  e.Tokens = <OneExtern2 <Names () e.Tokens>>;
}

OneExtern2 {
  (e.Names) SEMI e.Rest = (EXT e.Names) e.Rest;
}

Names {
  (e.Acc) (Ident e.N) e.R = <Names (e.Acc (Ident e.N)) e.R>;
  (e.Acc) COMMA (Ident e.N) e.R = <Names (e.Acc (Ident e.N)) e.R>;
  (e.Acc) e.Rest = (e.Acc) e.Rest;
}

OneFun {
  (e.V) (Ident e.N) e.Tokens = <OneFun2 (e.V) (Ident e.N) <FunBody e.Tokens>>;
}

OneFun2 {
  (e.V) (Ident e.N) (e.Sents) e.Rest = (FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents) e.Rest;
}

FunBody {
  LB e.Tokens = <Sentences () e.Tokens>;
}

Sentences {
  (e.Acc) RB e.Rest = (e.Acc) e.Rest;
  (e.Acc) e.Tokens = <SentencesJoin (e.Acc) <Sentence e.Tokens>>;
}

SentencesJoin {
  (e.Acc) (e.Sent) e.Rest = <Sentences (e.Acc (e.Sent)) e.Rest>;
}

Sentence {
  e.Tokens = <Sent1 <Terms () e.Tokens>>;
}

Sent1 {
  (e.Pat) COMMA e.Rest = <SentCond (e.Pat) () e.Rest>;
  (e.Pat) EQ e.Rest = <SentRes (e.Pat) () e.Rest>;
}

* Sent1 has already consumed the comma that separates the pattern from the
* conditions, so SentCond starts on the condition's expression.
SentCond {
  (e.Pat) (e.Conds) e.Rest = <SentCond2 (e.Pat) (e.Conds) <Terms () e.Rest>>;
}

SentCond2 {
  (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Expr) COLON e.Rest =
    <SentCond3 (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Expr) <Terms () e.Rest>>;
}

SentCond3 {
  (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Expr) () LB e.Rest =
    <SentCondBlk (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Expr) <Sentences () e.Rest>>;
  (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Expr) (e.CPat) COMMA e.Rest =
    <SentCond (e.Pat) (e.Conds (COND (e.Expr) (e.CPat))) e.Rest>;
  (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Expr) (e.CPat) EQ e.Rest =
    <SentRes (e.Pat) (e.Conds (COND (e.Expr) (e.CPat))) e.Rest>;
}

* A block in condition position: `, expression : { sentences }`. The block's
* argument is the condition's expression, as it is in the Rust AST.
SentCondBlk {
  (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Expr) (e.Sents) COMMA e.Rest =
    <SentCond (e.Pat) (e.Conds (COND (e.Expr) ((BLK (e.Expr) e.Sents)))) e.Rest>;
  (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Expr) (e.Sents) EQ e.Rest =
    <SentRes (e.Pat) (e.Conds (COND (e.Expr) ((BLK (e.Expr) e.Sents)))) e.Rest>;
}

SentRes {
  (e.Pat) (e.Conds) COMMA e.Rest = <SentResBlock (e.Pat) (e.Conds) e.Rest>;
  (e.Pat) (e.Conds) e.Rest = <SentRes2 (e.Pat) (e.Conds) <Terms () e.Rest>>;
}

* A sentence-ending block: `= , argument : { sentences } ;`
SentResBlock {
  (e.Pat) (e.Conds) e.Rest = <SentResBlock2 (e.Pat) (e.Conds) <Terms () e.Rest>>;
}

SentResBlock2 {
  (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Arg) COLON LB e.Rest =
    <SentResBlock3 (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Arg) <Sentences () e.Rest>>;
}

* The block's closing brace is consumed by Sentences; the sentence's own
* semicolon still has to be taken here, as it is in the plain path.
SentResBlock3 {
  (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Arg) (e.Sents) SEMI e.Rest =
    (SENT (e.Pat) (e.Conds) ((BLK (e.Arg) e.Sents))) e.Rest;
}

SentRes2 {
  (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Res) SEMI e.Rest = (SENT (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Res)) e.Rest;
}

* A term sequence stops at any token that cannot begin or continue a term.
Terms {
  (e.Acc) EQ e.R = (e.Acc) EQ e.R;
  (e.Acc) COMMA e.R = (e.Acc) COMMA e.R;
  (e.Acc) COLON e.R = (e.Acc) COLON e.R;
  (e.Acc) SEMI e.R = (e.Acc) SEMI e.R;
  (e.Acc) RB e.R = (e.Acc) RB e.R;
  (e.Acc) RP e.R = (e.Acc) RP e.R;
  (e.Acc) RA e.R = (e.Acc) RA e.R;
  (e.Acc) LB e.R = (e.Acc) LB e.R;
  (e.Acc) (Str e.C) e.R = <Terms (e.Acc <Chars e.C>) e.R>;
  (e.Acc) (Num e.D) e.R = <Terms (e.Acc (NUM e.D)) e.R>;
  (e.Acc) (Ident e.W) e.R = <Terms (e.Acc (ID e.W)) e.R>;
  (e.Acc) (Var e.K e.I) e.R = <Terms (e.Acc (VAR e.K e.I)) e.R>;
  (e.Acc) LP e.R = <TermsBr (e.Acc) e.R>;
  (e.Acc) LA e.R = <TermsCall (e.Acc) e.R>;
  (e.Acc) = (e.Acc);
}

* A quoted string is a sequence of character symbols, not one symbol.
Chars {
  = ;
  s.C e.R = (SYM s.C) <Chars e.R>;
}

TermsBr {
  (e.Acc) e.R = <TermsBr2 (e.Acc) <Terms () e.R>>;
}

TermsBr2 {
  (e.Acc) (e.Inner) RP e.R = <Terms (e.Acc (BR e.Inner)) e.R>;
}

TermsCall {
  (e.Acc) (Ident e.N) e.R = <TermsCall2 (e.Acc) (e.N) <Terms () e.R>>;
}

TermsCall2 {
  (e.Acc) (e.N) (e.Args) RA e.R = <Terms (e.Acc (CALL (ID e.N) e.Args)) e.R>;
}

* -------------------------------------------------------------- checker
*
* Prints OK when a program is well formed and a semicolon-separated diagnostic
* list otherwise. Checks today: the exported Go entry point, duplicate function
* names, and unbound variables in results.

Check {
  (PROG e.Items) = <Report <HasGo e.Items> <Dups (e.Items)> <Vars e.Items>>;
}

Report {
  = "OK";
  e.Msg = "ERRORS: " e.Msg;
}

* Refal-5 entry rule (reference A): execution starts from Go, and Go must be
* exported with $ENTRY.
* The parser stores a name as its characters, not as an identifier symbol, so
* the name is written here as the character literal 'Go'.
HasGo {
  (FUN (Ident 'Go') (VIS ENTRY) e.Sents) e.Rest = ;
  (FUN (Ident 'Go') (VIS LOCAL) e.Sents) e.Rest = "Go is defined but not exported with $ENTRY; ";
  t.Item e.Rest = <HasGo e.Rest>;
  = "the program must define an exported Go entry point; ";
}

* Duplicate function names.
*
* Detecting a duplicate is a question about a *set*, so it is answered by
* sorting: canonicalise each name once, sort the names, and look at neighbours.
* That is O(n log n).
*
* The pairwise pass below answers a different question -- it reports one message
* per ordered pair of equal names, so its *output* is quadratic in the number of
* colliding definitions and there is nothing to save there. It therefore runs
* only when the sorted scan has found a collision, which for a program the
* checker accepts is never.
*
* Why this matters and is not a micro-optimisation: the pairwise pass descends
* into both definitions' brackets for every pair, so on the compiler's own 201
* definitions it was 19.4 s of a 23.9 s self-hosting run -- 81% of it, and the
* only quadratic left in `Check`. Measured over synthetic corpora it is a clean
* O(n^2): 375 / 1425 / 6082 / 26062 ms at 50 / 100 / 200 / 400 definitions.
Dups {
  (e.Items) = <DupsWhen <AnyDup (<Sort <DupNameList (e.Items) ()>>)> (e.Items)>;
}

DupsWhen {
  () (e.Items) = ;
  (s.Any) (e.Items) = <DupsAll (e.Items)>;
}

* The canonical name of every function definition, in a list of its own.
* Names are compared under Classic Refal-5 name equivalence (reference 1.2.1):
* case folds and '-' is equivalent to '_'. FOO-BAR and FOO_BAR are one function.
DupNameList {
  () (e.Acc) = (e.Acc);
  ((FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents) e.Rest) (e.Acc) =
    <DupNameList (e.Rest) ((<Canon e.N>) e.Acc)>;
  (t.Item e.Rest) (e.Acc) = <DupNameList (e.Rest) (e.Acc)>;
}

* Merge sort. The list is split by dealing alternate elements, which needs no
* arithmetic on macrodigits and keeps every half the same length to within one.
*
* Convention, and it matters at every call: a function that takes a *list*
* takes it bracketed, and a function whose result is *spliced* into a larger
* expression returns the elements unwrapped. `Merge` is the second kind -- its
* result goes straight into `(e.K) <Merge ...>` -- so it returns an element
* sequence, and `Sort` brackets each half before handing it over. Getting this
* backwards costs a bracket level per merge, which reads as a wrong answer
* rather than as a crash.
Sort {
  () = ;
  ((e.X)) = (e.X);
  (e.L) = <Merge (<Sort <Fst <Split (e.L)>>>) (<Sort <Snd <Split (e.L)>>>)>;
}

Split {
  () = (() ());
  ((e.X)) = (((e.X)) ());
  ((e.A) (e.B) e.Rest) = <SplitJoin ((e.A)) ((e.B)) <Split (e.Rest)>>;
}

SplitJoin {
  (e.A) (e.B) ((e.X) (e.Y)) = ((e.A e.X) (e.B e.Y));
}

Merge {
  (e.A) () = e.A;
  () (e.B) = e.B;
  ((e.K) e.A) ((e.K2) e.B), <LeName (e.K) (e.K2)> : '1' =
    (e.K) <Merge (e.A) ((e.K2) e.B)>;
  ((e.K) e.A) ((e.K2) e.B) = (e.K2) <Merge ((e.K) e.A) (e.B)>;
}

Fst {
  ((e.A) (e.B)) = (e.A);
}

Snd {
  ((e.A) (e.B)) = (e.B);
}

LeName {
  () () = '1';
  () (e.B) = '1';
  (e.A) () = ;
  (s.X e.Rest) (s.Y e.More), <Compare <Ord s.X> <Ord s.Y>> : '-' = '1';
  (s.X e.Rest) (s.Y e.More), <Compare <Ord s.X> <Ord s.Y>> : '0' = <LeName (e.Rest) (e.More)>;
  (s.X e.Rest) (s.Y e.More) = ;
}

* True when two adjacent names in the sorted list are equal, which is exactly
* the condition under which a duplicate exists.
AnyDup {
  () = ();
  ((e.K)) = ();
  ((e.K) (e.K) e.Rest) = (1);
  ((e.K) (e.K2) e.Rest) = <AnyDup ((e.K2) e.Rest)>;
}

* One message per ordered pair of definitions with the same canonical name, in
* source order. Only reached when a collision exists.
DupsAll {
  () = ;
  ((FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents) e.Rest) =
    <DupName (e.N) (e.Rest)> <DupsAll (e.Rest)>;
  (t.Item e.Rest) = <DupsAll (e.Rest)>;
}

DupName {
  (e.N) () = ;
  (e.N) ((FUN (Ident e.M) (e.V) e.Sents) e.Rest),
    <EqName (<Canon e.N>) (<Canon e.M>)> : '1' = "duplicate function " e.M "; ";
  (e.N) (t.Item e.Rest) = <DupName (e.N) (e.Rest)>;
}

EqName {
  (e.A) (e.A) = '1';
  (e.A) (e.B) = ;
}

Canon {
  = ;
  '-' e.R = '_' <Canon e.R>;
  s.C e.R = <CanonChar s.C> <Canon e.R>;
}

* Folds A-Z to a-z by arithmetic rather than 26 sentences per case. Compare
* returns -, 0 or +, so the two tests are "below A" and "within A-Z".
CanonChar {
  s.C, <Ord s.C> : s.N, <Compare s.N 65> : '-' = s.C;
  s.C, <Ord s.C> : s.N, <Compare s.N 91> : '-' = <Chr <Add s.N 32>>;
  s.C = s.C;
}

Vars {
  = ;
  (FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents) e.Rest = <VarsSents e.Sents> <Vars e.Rest>;
  t.Item e.Rest = <Vars e.Rest>;
}

VarsSents {
  = ;
  (SENT (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Res)) e.Rest = <VarsSent (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Res)> <VarsSents e.Rest>;
  t.Item e.Rest = <VarsSents e.Rest>;
}

* A result variable must be bound by the sentence pattern or by the pattern of
* one of its conditions.
VarsSent {
  (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Res) = <Unbound (e.Res) (<VarsOf e.Pat> <CondVars e.Conds>)>;
}

CondVars {
  = ;
  (COND (e.Expr) (e.CPat)) e.Rest = <VarsOf e.CPat> <CondVars e.Rest>;
}

* A variable record carries BOTH spellings: the source spelling is what a
* diagnostic must print, and the canonical spelling is what two variables must
* be compared by. Reference 1.3 makes e.Text and e.text the same variable, but
* the Rust bootstrap still reports `unbound variable e.Missing` as written, so
* folding the index at capture time silently corrupted the message.
VarsOf {
  = ;
  (VAR s.K e.I) e.Rest = (V s.K (e.I) (<Canon e.I>)) <VarsOf e.Rest>;
  (BR e.Inner) e.Rest = <VarsOf e.Inner> <VarsOf e.Rest>;
  (CALL (ID e.N) e.Args) e.Rest = <VarsOf e.Args> <VarsOf e.Rest>;
  t.Item e.Rest = <VarsOf e.Rest>;
}

Unbound {
  (e.Res) (e.Bound) = <UnboundVars <VarsOf e.Res> (e.Bound)>;
}

UnboundVars {
  (e.Bound) = ;
  (V s.K (e.I) (e.C)) e.Rest (e.Bound),
    <IsBound (V s.K (e.I) (e.C)) (e.Bound)> : '1' =
      <UnboundVars e.Rest (e.Bound)>;
  (V s.K (e.I) (e.C)) e.Rest (e.Bound) =
    "unbound variable " s.K "." e.I "; " <UnboundVars e.Rest (e.Bound)>;
}

* A bound record is a bracket holding several atoms (V, the kind, and the index
* characters), so it cannot be matched with `(t.V)`, which is a bracket holding
* exactly one term. Compare bracket contents instead.
* `e.V` holds the CONTENTS of the variable bracket, so the comparison has to
* re-wrap it as `(e.V)` before it can be matched against the bound record.
IsBound {
  (e.V) (t.Item e.Rest), <EqVar t.Item (e.V)> : '1' = '1';
  (e.V) (t.Item e.Rest) = <IsBound (e.V) (e.Rest)>;
  (e.V) () = ;
}

* Two variables are the same when the kind matches and the CANONICAL indices
* match. The source spellings deliberately take no part in the comparison.
EqVar {
  (V s.K (e.I) (e.C)) (V s.K (e.J) (e.C)) = '1';
  (e.A) (e.B) = ;
}

* --------------------------------------------------------------- emitter
*
* Emits Core Refal matching the Rust bootstrap's `refal lower` byte for byte:
*   $EXTERN a, b;            then a blank line, per declaration
*   $ENTRY Name {            sentences indented two spaces
*     pattern, expr : pat = result;
*   }                        a blank line between functions, one newline after

NL {
  = <Chr 10>;
}

Emit {
  (PROG e.Items) = <EmitDecls e.Items> <EmitFnList <OnlyFuns e.Items>>;
}

EmitDecls {
  = ;
  (EXT e.Names) e.Rest = "$EXTERN " <NameList e.Names> ";" <NL> <NL> <EmitDecls e.Rest>;
  t.Item e.Rest = <EmitDecls e.Rest>;
}

NameList {
  (Ident e.N) = e.N;
  (Ident e.N) e.Rest = e.N ", " <NameList e.Rest>;
}

OnlyFuns {
  = ;
  (FUN e.Body) e.Rest = (FUN e.Body) <OnlyFuns e.Rest>;
  t.Item e.Rest = <OnlyFuns e.Rest>;
}

* `refal lower` prints with print!, not println!, so the formatted program ends
* at the last closing brace with no trailing newline. Prout supplies the final
* newline, so the emitter must stop at '}' and put the blank line that separates
* two functions only between them.
EmitFnList {
  = ;
  (FUN e.Body) e.Rest = <EmitFun e.Body> <IfMore e.Rest>;
}

IfMore {
  = ;
  e.Rest = <NL> <NL> <EmitFnList e.Rest>;
}

EmitFun {
  (Ident e.N) (VIS ENTRY) e.Sents = "$ENTRY " e.N " {" <NL> <EmitSents ("  ") e.Sents> "}";
  (Ident e.N) (VIS LOCAL) e.Sents = e.N " {" <NL> <EmitSents ("  ") e.Sents> "}";
}

* Indentation is carried as a run of spaces rather than a count, so a nested
* block simply widens it by two and nesting works to any depth.
EmitSents {
  (e.Ind) = ;
  (e.Ind) (SENT (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Res)) e.Rest =
    e.Ind <EmitTerms e.Pat> <EmitConds (e.Ind) e.Conds>
    <EmitSpace (e.Pat) (e.Conds)> "=" <EmitResult (e.Ind) (e.Res)> ";" <NL>
    <EmitSents (e.Ind) e.Rest>;
}

* A space before the = only when the pattern or a condition precedes it.
EmitSpace {
  () () = ;
  (e.Pat) (e.Conds) = " ";
}

* A result that is exactly one block renders as `, argument : { ... }`.
EmitResult {
  (e.Ind) () = ;
  (e.Ind) ((BLK (e.Arg) e.Sents)) =
    " ," <EmitBlkArg (e.Arg)> " : {" <NL>
    <EmitSents (e.Ind "  ") e.Sents> e.Ind "}";
  (e.Ind) (e.Res) = " " <EmitTerms e.Res>;
}

EmitBlkArg {
  () = ;
  (e.Arg) = " " <EmitTerms e.Arg>;
}

* A condition whose pattern is a block renders its body rather than terms.
EmitConds {
  (e.Ind) = ;
  (e.Ind) () = ;
  (e.Ind) (COND (e.Expr) ((BLK (e.BArg) e.Sents))) e.Rest =
    ", " <EmitTerms e.Expr> " : {" <NL>
    <EmitSents (e.Ind "  ") e.Sents> e.Ind "}" <EmitConds (e.Ind) e.Rest>;
  (e.Ind) (COND (e.Expr) (e.CPat)) e.Rest =
    ", " <EmitTerms e.Expr> " : " <EmitTerms e.CPat> <EmitConds (e.Ind) e.Rest>;
}

EmitTerms {
  = ;
  (SYM s.C) = <EmitChar s.C>;
  (SYM s.C) e.Rest = <EmitChar s.C> " " <EmitTerms e.Rest>;
  (ID e.N) = e.N;
  (ID e.N) e.Rest = e.N " " <EmitTerms e.Rest>;
  (NUM e.D) = e.D;
  (NUM e.D) e.Rest = e.D " " <EmitTerms e.Rest>;
  (VAR s.K e.I) = s.K "." e.I;
  (VAR s.K e.I) e.Rest = s.K "." e.I " " <EmitTerms e.Rest>;
  (BR e.Inner) = "(" <EmitTerms e.Inner> ")";
  (BR e.Inner) e.Rest = "(" <EmitTerms e.Inner> ") " <EmitTerms e.Rest>;
  (CALL (ID e.N) e.Args) = "<" e.N <EmitCallArgs e.Args> ">";
  (CALL (ID e.N) e.Args) e.Rest = "<" e.N <EmitCallArgs e.Args> "> " <EmitTerms e.Rest>;
}

EmitCallArgs {
  = ;
  e.Args = " " <EmitTerms e.Args>;
}

* A character is quoted with ' unless it is itself a quote, and then with ".
EmitChar {
  "'" = '"' "'" '"';
  s.C = "'" s.C "'";
}

* ------------------------------------------------------------------ graph
*
* Turchin 1980 4.2, the graph of states, built in Refal and printed
* byte-identically to the Rust bootstrap's `refal graph`. A state is a
* sentence, in program order; a transition is one call occurrence whose callee
* names a function that appears in the program; the entry is the state of Go.
* `refal graph` prints the graph after clean_unreachable_states, so the
* reachability cleanup and the renumbering it implies are reproduced here too,
* which is what makes the two comparable byte for byte.
*
* This is the first piece of the *transforming* half of the compiler -- the
* half Turchin's Chapter 4 makes the centre of compilation -- to be written in
* Refal rather than in refal-core. It reuses Lex and Parse exactly as the
* checker and the emitter do, so nothing is restated.
*
* One convention runs through the section, because getting it wrong fails
* silently: a function that walks a list takes the list SPREAD (one argument
* per element) and separates its base case by arity, while a function that
* wants a list as a single value takes it as one bracketed argument and
* destructures it with `(e.X)`. Choosing the wrong one binds the first element
* where the whole list was meant, which is a wrong answer rather than an error.

Graph {
  (PROG e.Items) = <FormatGraph <CleanG <BuildG e.Items>>>;
}

* ---- build the seed graph

BuildG {
  e.Items = <BuildG2 (<FlatStates e.Items>)>;
}

* One record per sentence, in program order: (name characters, sentence index,
* the sentence itself). The item is taken whole and destructured in FlatItem,
* because `e.Sents e.Rest` would be two adjacent e. variables and split
* shortest-first, leaving the recursion no argument to consume.
FlatStates {
  = ;
  t.Item e.Rest = <FlatItem t.Item> <FlatStates e.Rest>;
}

* A non-function item ($EXTERN) contributes no state and must not stall the
* walk, so the second sentence accepts any bracket at all.
FlatItem {
  (FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents) = <SPairs (e.N) 0 e.Sents>;
  (e.Any) = ;
}

SPairs {
  (e.N) s.I = ;
  (e.N) s.I t.Sent e.Rest = ((e.N) s.I t.Sent) <SPairs (e.N) <Add s.I 1> e.Rest>;
}

BuildG2 {
  (e.Pairs) = <BuildG3 (<StatesOf (e.Pairs)>) (<FirstsOf (e.Pairs)>)>;
}

* States carry ids 0,1,2... in program order.
StatesOf {
  (e.Pairs) = <StatesOf2 0 e.Pairs>;
}

StatesOf2 {
  s.I = ;
  s.I ((e.N) s.J t.Sent) e.Rest = (ST s.I (e.N) s.J t.Sent) <StatesOf2 <Add s.I 1> e.Rest>;
}

BuildG3 {
  (e.States) (e.Firsts) =
    (GR (<LookupGo (e.Firsts)>) (e.States) (<TransOf (e.States) (e.Firsts)>));
}

* first_states[uppercase(name)] = the function's first state id. A later
* function with the same name does not overwrite it, so the accumulator only
* ever grows and the lookup decides.
FirstsOf {
  (e.Pairs) = <FirstsL () <StatesOf (e.Pairs)>>;
}

FirstsL {
  (e.Acc) = e.Acc;
  (e.Acc) (ST s.Id (e.N) s.J t.Sent) e.Rest =
    <FirstsL <FirstAdd (e.Acc) (<Upper e.N>) s.Id> e.Rest>;
}

FirstAdd {
  (e.Acc) (e.U) s.Id = <FirstAdd2 (e.Acc) (e.U) s.Id <Lookup (e.Acc) (e.U)>>;
}

FirstAdd2 {
  (e.Acc) (e.U) s.Id s.P = (e.Acc);
  (e.Acc) (e.U) s.Id = (e.Acc ((e.U) s.Id));
}

Lookup {
  (e.Acc) (e.Q) = <LookupL (e.Q) e.Acc>;
}

* Key equality is exact after uppercasing, which is all build_seed_graph asks
* for: to_ascii_uppercase, with no '-'/'_' folding.
*
* The query is the *first* argument and the table the tail, and that is not
* cosmetic. Written the other way round -- table spread, query last -- the
* pattern is `((e.U) s.Id) e.Rest (e.Q)`, where a trailing bracket sits after an
* expression variable: the matcher cannot know where `e.Rest` ends without
* walking to the end of the list, so one step of the scan costs O(n) and a
* single lookup costs O(n^2). With the query in front every position is
* determinate -- two one-term bindings and a remainder -- so a step is O(1) and
* a lookup is O(n). The table is 479 entries on this compiler and the pass
* performs one lookup per call occurrence, so the difference is the difference
* between a second and a minute.
LookupL {
  (e.Q) = ;
  (e.Q) ((e.U) s.Id) e.Rest, <SameChars (e.U) (e.Q)> : '1' = s.Id;
  (e.Q) ((e.F) s.Id) e.Rest = <LookupL (e.Q) e.Rest>;
}

LookupGo {
  (e.Firsts) = <LookupGoL e.Firsts>;
}

LookupGoL {
  = ;
  ((e.U) s.Id) e.Rest, <SameChars (e.U) ('GO')> : '1' = s.Id;
  (e.F) e.Rest = <LookupGoL e.Rest>;
}

SameChars {
  (e.A) (e.A) = '1';
  (e.A) (e.B) = ;
}

* ---- transitions: one per call occurrence, in term order

TransOf {
  (e.States) (e.Firsts) = <TransOf2 (e.Firsts) e.States>;
}

TransOf2 {
  (e.Firsts) = ;
  (e.Firsts) (ST s.Id (e.N) s.J t.Sent) e.Rest =
    <StateTrans s.Id (e.Firsts) <SentenceCalls t.Sent>> <TransOf2 (e.Firsts) e.Rest>;
}

StateTrans {
  s.Id (e.Firsts) = ;
  s.Id (e.Firsts) (e.Callee) e.Rest =
    <EmitTrans s.Id (e.Firsts) (e.Callee)> <StateTrans s.Id (e.Firsts) e.Rest>;
}

EmitTrans {
  s.Id (e.Firsts) (e.Callee) =
    <EmitTrans2 s.Id (e.Callee) <Lookup (e.Firsts) (<Upper e.Callee>)>>;
}

* A transition exists only when the callee names a function in the program, so
* a call to anything else produces none, and two identical calls are not
* deduplicated. The callee is emitted as written, not uppercased.
EmitTrans2 {
  s.Id (e.Callee) s.To = (TR s.Id (e.Callee) s.To);
  s.Id (e.Callee) = ;
}

* The order is the one collect_graph_state_call_names uses: the pattern, then
* for each condition its expression and then its pattern, then the result.
SentenceCalls {
  (SENT (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Res)) = <TermCalls e.Pat> <CondCalls e.Conds> <TermCalls e.Res>;
}

CondCalls {
  = ;
  (COND (e.Expr) (e.CPat)) e.Rest = <TermCalls e.Expr> <TermCalls e.CPat> <CondCalls e.Rest>;
}

TermCalls {
  = ;
  t.Term e.Rest = <OneTermCalls t.Term> <TermCalls e.Rest>;
}

OneTermCalls {
  (SYM s.C) = ;
  (ID e.N) = ;
  (NUM e.D) = ;
  (VAR s.K e.I) = ;
  (BR e.Inner) = <TermCalls e.Inner>;
  (BLK (e.Arg) e.Sents) = <TermCalls e.Arg> <SentCalls e.Sents>;
  (CALL (ID e.N) e.Args) = (e.N) <TermCalls e.Args>;
  t.Other = ;
}

SentCalls {
  = ;
  t.Sent e.Rest = <SentenceCalls t.Sent> <SentCalls e.Rest>;
}

* ---- clean: keep only what the entry can reach (clean_unreachable_states)
*
* The Rust walks a BFS with a HashSet and reads `states[state.0]` by index. The
* list transcription of that walk -- a visited-list scan, plus a full scan of
* the state list and of the transition list for every dequeued state -- costs
* O(n * (n + m)) steps with a function call at each one, and on the compiler's
* own 1,094-state graph it was 462 s of the 480 s graph pass.
*
* Two properties of the walk remove the scans. From a state it reaches *every*
* state of the same function, so the reachable set is a union of whole
* functions. And BuildG emits a transition's target as
* `first_states[Upper callee]`, while `first_states` maps a name to the
* function's *first* state id -- so a transition's target id *is* the function's
* canonical id. Reachability is therefore reachability on the call graph, whose
* nodes are canonical ids: 480 of them rather than 1,094 states, compared by
* symbol equality rather than by string comparison.
*
* Every list this pass joins is already ordered by the key it is joined on --
* states by id, transitions by source id -- so each join below is a merge join.
* The one list that is out of order is the name -> canonical-id map, and a merge
* sort restores it.

CleanG {
  (GR (e.Entry) (e.States) (e.Trans)) = <CleanG2 (e.Entry) (e.States) (e.Trans)>;
}

CleanG2 {
  () (e.States) (e.Trans) = (CG () () ());
  (s.E) (e.States) (e.Trans) = <CleanG3 (e.States) (e.Trans) s.E (<GidOf (e.States)>)>;
}

CleanG3 {
  (e.States) (e.Trans) s.E (e.GI) =
    <CleanG4 (e.States) (e.Trans) s.E (e.GI)
      (<GReach <GAdj (e.GI) (e.Trans)> <GidAt s.E e.GI>>)>;
}

CleanG4 {
  (e.States) (e.Trans) s.E (e.GI) (e.RG) =
    <Renumber2 (e.Trans) s.E (e.RG) <RenumS 0 () () (<ReachIds (e.GI) (e.RG)>) e.States>>;
}

* ---- the canonical id of a function

* One pair per state, key first so the merge sort can use the name comparator.
GidOf {
  (e.States) = <GidOf3 (<GidOf2 (<SortName (<GidOf1 e.States>)>)>)>;
}

GidOf1 {
  = ;
  (ST s.Id (e.N) s.J t.Sent) e.Rest = ((<Upper e.N>) s.Id) <GidOf1 e.Rest>;
}

* Equal names are adjacent after the sort, so one run is one function and its
* canonical id is the smallest id in the run -- which is what first_states
* records, the state list being in program order.
*
* The sort keys on the name alone, so the ids inside a run are not ordered; the
* run's minimum is therefore found first and then stamped on every member. A
* running minimum would have to be revised after members had already been
* emitted, which is the one thing a single pass cannot do.
GidOf2 {
  () = ;
  (((e.U) s.Id) e.Rest) =
    <GidOfStamp (e.U) (((e.U) s.Id) e.Rest) <GidOfMin (e.U) (((e.U) s.Id) e.Rest) s.Id>>;
}

* The run's smallest id, stopping at the first name that differs.
GidOfMin {
  (e.U) () s.Min = s.Min;
  (e.U) (((e.U) s.Id) e.Rest) s.Min, <Compare s.Id s.Min> : '-' =
    <GidOfMin (e.U) (e.Rest) s.Id>;
  (e.U) (((e.U) s.Id) e.Rest) s.Min = <GidOfMin (e.U) (e.Rest) s.Min>;
  (e.U) (((e.V) s.Id) e.Rest) s.Min = s.Min;
}

* One pair per member of the run, then the next run.
GidOfStamp {
  (e.U) () s.Min = ;
  (e.U) (((e.U) s.Id) e.Rest) s.Min =
    ((s.Id) s.Min) <GidOfStamp (e.U) (e.Rest) s.Min>;
  (e.U) (((e.V) s.Id) e.Rest) s.Min = <GidOf2 (((e.V) s.Id) e.Rest)>;
}

* Back into id order: the id leads the pair, so the numeric sort is the key.
GidOf3 {
  (e.Pairs) = <GidOf4 (<SortN (e.Pairs)>)>;
}

GidOf4 {
  () = ;
  (((s.Id) s.G) e.Rest) = (GI s.Id s.G) <GidOf4 (e.Rest)>;
}

* The entry's canonical id. The query leads and the map follows it spread, so
* every position in the pattern is determinate; the map is in id order, so this
* walks once.
GidAt {
  s.E (GI s.E s.G) e.Rest = (s.G);
  s.E (GI s.Id s.G) e.Rest = <GidAt s.E e.Rest>;
  s.E = ();
}

* ---- the call graph

* One edge per transition. The source group comes from the id-ordered map by a
* merge join -- both lists ascend by id -- and the target group is the
* transition's own `s.To`, which is a canonical id by construction, so no
* lookup is performed at all.
GAdj {
  (e.GI) (e.Trans) = <GAdj2 (<GEdges (e.GI) (e.Trans)>)>;
}

GEdges {
  (e.GI) () = ;
  ((GI s.Id s.G) e.GI2) ((TR s.Id (e.Callee) s.To) e.Rest) =
    (GE s.G s.To) <GEdges ((GI s.Id s.G) e.GI2) (e.Rest)>;
  ((GI s.Id s.G) e.GI2) ((TR s.F (e.Callee) s.To) e.Rest), <Compare s.F s.Id> : '+' =
    <GEdges (e.GI2) ((TR s.F (e.Callee) s.To) e.Rest)>;
}

* Sorted by source group, so a group's targets are one contiguous run.
GAdj2 {
  (e.Edges) = (<GAdj3 (<SortN (<GAdj4 e.Edges>)>)>);
}

GAdj4 {
  = ;
  (GE s.From s.To) e.Rest = ((s.From) s.To) <GAdj4 e.Rest>;
}

GAdj3 {
  () = ;
  (((s.From) s.To) e.Rest) = (GE s.From s.To) <GAdj3 (e.Rest)>;
}

* ---- reachability on the call graph

GReach {
  (e.Adj) () = ();
  (e.Adj) (s.E) = <GReachSort <GReachLoop (e.Adj) (s.E) ()>>;
}

* The visited set comes out in discovery order, and the join that consumes it in
* ReachIds needs it ascending, so it is sorted once here rather than walked once
* per state there.
GReachSort {
  (e.Seen) = <GReachSort2 (<SortN (<GReachSort1 e.Seen>)>)>;
}

GReachSort1 {
  = ;
  s.G e.Rest = ((s.G)) <GReachSort1 e.Rest>;
}

GReachSort2 {
  () = ;
  (((s.G)) e.Rest) = s.G <GReachSort2 (e.Rest)>;
}

GReachLoop {
  (e.Adj) () (e.Seen) = (e.Seen);
  (e.Adj) (s.G e.More) (e.Seen), <Member s.G (e.Seen)> : '1' =
    <GReachLoop (e.Adj) (e.More) (e.Seen)>;
  (e.Adj) (s.G e.More) (e.Seen) =
    <GReachLoop (e.Adj) <Enqueue (e.More) <GTargets (e.Adj) s.G>> (s.G e.Seen)>;
}

Enqueue {
  (e.Q) = (e.Q);
  (e.Q) s.X e.Rest = <Enqueue (e.Q s.X) e.Rest>;
}

GTargets {
  (e.Adj) s.G = <GTargets2 s.G (e.Adj)>;
}

* The adjacency list is sorted by source group, so this stops as soon as it has
* passed the group's run and never walks the whole list for a late group.
GTargets2 {
  s.G () = ;
  s.G ((GE s.G s.To) e.Rest) = s.To <GTargets2 s.G (e.Rest)>;
  s.G ((GE s.H s.To) e.Rest), <Compare s.G s.H> : '-' = ;
  s.G ((GE s.H s.To) e.Rest) = <GTargets2 s.G (e.Rest)>;
}

* ---- the reachable states

* Sorting the group map by group makes the membership test a merge join against
* the reachable groups, instead of a walk of the reachable set per state.
ReachIds {
  (e.GI) (e.RG) = <ReachIds3 (<ReachIds2 (<SortN (<ReachIds1 e.GI>)>) (e.RG)>)>;
}

ReachIds1 {
  = ;
  (GI s.Id s.G) e.Rest = ((s.G) s.Id) <ReachIds1 e.Rest>;
}

ReachIds2 {
  () (e.RG) = ;
  (e.Pairs) () = ;
  (((s.G) s.Id) e.Rest) (s.G e.RG) = ((s.Id)) <ReachIds2 (e.Rest) (s.G e.RG)>;
  (((s.G) s.Id) e.Rest) (s.H e.RG), <Compare s.G s.H> : '+' =
    <ReachIds2 (((s.G) s.Id) e.Rest) (e.RG)>;
  (((s.G) s.Id) e.Rest) (e.RG) = <ReachIds2 (e.Rest) (e.RG)>;
}

ReachIds3 {
  (e.Ids) = <ReachIds4 (<SortN (e.Ids)>)>;
}

ReachIds4 {
  () = ;
  (((s.Id)) e.Rest) = s.Id <ReachIds4 (e.Rest)>;
}

* ---- merge sorts

* `Sort` and `Merge` key on the whole element, so a pair needs its own
* comparator. The shape is otherwise the same, including the convention that a
* merge returns its elements unwrapped and its callers bracket each half.

SortName {
  () = ;
  ((e.X)) = (e.X);
  (e.L) = <MergeName (<SortName <Fst <Split (e.L)>>>) (<SortName <Snd <Split (e.L)>>>)>;
}

MergeName {
  (e.A) () = e.A;
  () (e.B) = e.B;
  ((e.K) e.A) ((e.K2) e.B), <LePair (e.K) (e.K2)> : '1' =
    (e.K) <MergeName (e.A) ((e.K2) e.B)>;
  ((e.K) e.A) ((e.K2) e.B) = (e.K2) <MergeName ((e.K) e.A) (e.B)>;
}

* An element is ((name) id): the name is the key and the id travels with it.
LePair {
  ((e.A) s.Id) ((e.B) s.J) = <LeName (e.A) (e.B)>;
}

SortN {
  () = ;
  ((e.X)) = (e.X);
  (e.L) = <MergeN (<SortN <Fst <Split (e.L)>>>) (<SortN <Snd <Split (e.L)>>>)>;
}

MergeN {
  (e.A) () = e.A;
  () (e.B) = e.B;
  ((e.K) e.A) ((e.K2) e.B), <LeNum (e.K) (e.K2)> : '1' =
    (e.K) <MergeN (e.A) ((e.K2) e.B)>;
  ((e.K) e.A) ((e.K2) e.B) = (e.K2) <MergeN ((e.K) e.A) (e.B)>;
}

* The key is the number that leads the element; whatever follows it is payload.
LeNum {
  ((s.A) e.T) ((s.B) e.U), <Compare s.A s.B> : '-' = '1';
  ((s.A) e.T) ((s.B) e.U) = ;
}

Member {
  s.X (e.S) = <MemberL s.X e.S>;
}

MemberL {
  s.X = ;
  s.X s.X e.Rest = '1';
  s.X s.Y e.Rest = <MemberL s.X e.Rest>;
}

* Renumber the reachable states 0,1,2... in program order and carry the
* remapping into the transitions; an edge with an unmapped endpoint is the one
* that led to a state which was removed.
*
* `Reach` is sorted ascending and the states are in id order, so the two move
* together: the membership test that used to walk the reachable set once per
* state is now one step per state.
RenumS {
  s.Next (e.Map) (e.New) () = (RS (e.Map) (e.New));
  s.Next (e.Map) (e.New) (s.Id e.Reach) (ST s.Id (e.N) s.J t.Sent) e.Rest =
    <RenumS <Add s.Next 1> (e.Map (RM s.Id s.Next)) (e.New (ST s.Next (e.N) s.J t.Sent))
      (e.Reach) e.Rest>;
  s.Next (e.Map) (e.New) (e.Reach) (ST s.Id (e.N) s.J t.Sent) e.Rest =
    <RenumS s.Next (e.Map) (e.New) (e.Reach) e.Rest>;
}

Renumber2 {
  (e.Trans) s.E (e.RG) (RS (e.Map) (e.New)) =
    (CG (<LookupM (e.Map) s.E>) (e.New)
        (<RenumT (e.Map) (<HeadMap (e.Map) (e.RG)>) (e.Trans)>));
}

LookupM {
  (e.Map) s.X = <LookupML s.X e.Map>;
}

* The query is the first argument and the map the tail, for the same reason
* Lookup takes its query first: written the other way round the pattern ends
* `e.Rest s.X`, an expression variable followed by a term, and the matcher has
* to walk the rest of the map to find where e.Rest stops.
LookupML {
  s.X = ;
  s.X (RM s.X s.N) e.Rest = s.N;
  s.X (e.R) e.Rest = <LookupML s.X e.Rest>;
}

* A transition's target is a canonical id -- a group head -- so the target remap
* needs one entry per function rather than one per state. Both lists ascend by
* old id, so this is a merge join.
HeadMap {
  (e.Map) (e.RG) = <HeadMap2 (e.Map) (e.RG)>;
}

HeadMap2 {
  (e.Map) () = ;
  () (e.RG) = ;
  ((RM s.Id s.N) e.Rest) (s.Id e.RG) = (HM s.Id s.N) <HeadMap2 (e.Rest) (s.Id e.RG)>;
  ((RM s.Id s.N) e.Rest) (s.H e.RG), <Compare s.Id s.H> : '+' =
    <HeadMap2 ((RM s.Id s.N) e.Rest) (e.RG)>;
  ((RM s.Id s.N) e.Rest) (e.RG) = <HeadMap2 (e.Rest) (e.RG)>;
}

* The transitions are in source-id order and the map is in old-id order, so the
* source remap is a merge join; the target remap is a lookup in the head map,
* which has one entry per function rather than one per state.
RenumT {
  (e.Map) (e.HM) () = ;
  ((RM s.F s.NF) e.Map2) (e.HM) ((TR s.F (e.Callee) s.T) e.Rest),
    <MapTo s.T e.HM> : s.NT =
      (TR s.NF (e.Callee) s.NT) <RenumT ((RM s.F s.NF) e.Map2) (e.HM) (e.Rest)>;
  ((RM s.F s.NF) e.Map2) (e.HM) ((TR s.G (e.Callee) s.T) e.Rest), <Compare s.G s.F> : '+' =
    <RenumT (e.Map2) (e.HM) ((TR s.G (e.Callee) s.T) e.Rest)>;
  (e.Map) (e.HM) ((e.T) e.Rest) = <RenumT (e.Map) (e.HM) (e.Rest)>;
}

MapTo {
  s.E (HM s.E s.N) e.Rest = s.N;
  s.E (HM s.Id s.N) e.Rest = <MapTo s.E e.Rest>;
  s.E = ;
}

* ---- print: `format_seed_graph`, with the final newline left to Prout

FormatGraph {
  (CG () (e.States) (e.Trans)) = "entry: <none>" <FmtTail (e.States) (e.Trans)>;
  (CG (s.E) (e.States) (e.Trans)) = "entry: S" s.E <FmtTail (e.States) (e.Trans)>;
}

FmtTail {
  (e.States) (e.Trans) = <FmtStates e.States> <FmtTrans e.Trans>;
}

FmtStates {
  = ;
  (ST s.Id (e.N) s.J t.Sent) e.Rest = <NL> "S" s.Id " = " e.N "#" s.J <FmtStates e.Rest>;
}

FmtTrans {
  = ;
  (TR s.F (e.Callee) s.T) e.Rest = <NL> "S" s.F " -" e.Callee "-> S" s.T <FmtTrans e.Rest>;
}

* ------------------------------------------------------------- residualize
*
* The cleaned graph denotes a program, and emitting that program is the first
* stage of the transforming half that produces *source* rather than a report.
* `refal residualize-graph` is lower -> build_seed_graph ->
* clean_unreachable_states -> residualize_cleaned_graph -> format_program, and
* this reproduces the last two steps on top of the Graph stage above, reusing the
* emitter that already matches `refal lower` byte for byte.
*
* residualize_cleaned_graph walks the ORIGINAL program's functions in order and
* keeps, for each, the surviving states whose function name matches
* case-insensitively. A function with no surviving sentence disappears, which is
* the reachability dead-code elimination that cleaning performed: in
* `metacode-chapter6.ref` the function Echo is named only inside a quoted string,
* nothing calls it, and it is not in the residue. Declarations ($EXTERN) are
* carried through untouched, and a function's visibility comes from the program
* rather than from the graph, because a state does not record it.
*
* The sentences come out in state order, which is program order, which is the
* span order residualize_cleaned_graph sorts by. So the rebuilt PROG differs from
* the source PROG in exactly one respect -- the functions cleaning removed -- and
* the emitter renders it byte-identically to `refal residualize-graph`.

Residualize {
  (PROG e.Items) = <Emit (PROG <ResidItems (<CleanG <BuildG e.Items>>) e.Items>)>;
}

* The graph is carried bracketed and last, because a function that survives needs
* it again for the next item. A list returned unwrapped spreads across the
* caller's arguments -- the bug that hid the seed graph's firsts table.
ResidItems {
  (e.G) = ;
  (e.G) (EXT e.Names) e.Rest = (EXT e.Names) <ResidItems (e.G) e.Rest>;
  (e.G) (FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents) e.Rest =
    <ResidFun (e.N) (e.V) (e.G) (e.Rest)>;
  (e.G) t.Other e.Rest = <ResidItems (e.G) e.Rest>;
}

ResidFun {
  (e.N) (e.V) (e.G) (e.Rest) =
    <ResidFun2 (e.N) (e.V) (e.G) (e.Rest) <ResidSents (e.N) e.G>>;
}

* A function whose every sentence cleaning removed is not emitted at all: its
* sentences are gone from the graph, so there is nothing left to print. An empty
* FUN would not be Refal, which is why the case is separate from the general one.
* The surviving sentences arrive *bracketed*, because an unbracketed list of
* length zero is indistinguishable from an absent argument -- `() ` would then
* never match and every dropped function would come out as an empty `F { }`.
ResidFun2 {
  (e.N) (e.V) (e.G) (e.Rest) () = <ResidItems (e.G) e.Rest>;
  (e.N) (e.V) (e.G) (e.Rest) (e.Sents) =
    (FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents) <ResidItems (e.G) e.Rest>;
}

ResidSents {
  (e.N) (CG (e.Entry) (e.States) (e.Trans)) = (<ResidSents2 (<Upper e.N>) e.States>);
}

* Name equality is exact after uppercasing, which is what
* residualize_cleaned_graph asks for with eq_ignore_ascii_case on ASCII names.
ResidSents2 {
  (e.U) = ;
  (e.U) (ST s.Id (e.M) s.J t.Sent) e.Rest, <SameChars (<Upper e.M>) (e.U)> : '1' =
    t.Sent <ResidSents2 (e.U) e.Rest>;
  (e.U) (e.S) e.Rest = <ResidSents2 (e.U) e.Rest>;
}

* ------------------------------------------------------------------ drive
*
* Section 4.2's other half. The graph says which sentences exist; driving says
* which of them a configuration actually reaches, by contracting calls until
* nothing is left to contract. This reproduces `refal drive`, which is
* drive_ground over the closed entry configuration <Go>: every selected sentence
* is recorded as a visited state, and the fully instantiated result is printed.
*
* The semantics are refal-core's, not the runtime's, and one difference is
* visible in the output: Prout is *not* a printer here, it is the identity.
* drive_ground special-cases it to return its argument, so `output:` is the
* value the program computed rather than what it printed.
*
* Two conventions run through the section, both of them responses to traps this
* repository has paid for.
*
* 1. Every list is passed BRACKETED. A function that appends to a list can then
*    do so without an adjacent e-variable splitting shortest-first and binding
*    empty, which is the silent hang; and "no list" is a different shape from
*    "empty list" when it matters.
* 2. Every computed result is passed FIRST, never last after a spread list. A
*    trailing spread list followed by a pattern term is matched by trying the
*    shortest split first, which works but backtracks over the whole list; a
*    result in first position cannot be misread at all.
*
* The step counter is threaded through *failures* as well as successes, because
* that is what the Rust driver does: `match_ground_conditions` mutates `self`
* before it returns false, so a sentence whose condition fails still costs the
* steps its condition expression spent. `condition-block.ref` is the case that
* makes this observable -- its condition calls `Approve`, and the reported step
* count only comes out right if that call is counted once per attempt.

Drive {
  (PROG e.Items) = <DriveRun <CleanG <BuildG e.Items>>>;
}

DriveRun {
  (CG () (e.States) (e.Trans)) = "entry: <none>";
  (CG (s.E) (e.States) (e.Trans)) =
    <DriveEntry (e.States) <DvNameOf s.E (e.States)>>;
}

* The entry is a state id; the driver needs the function that state belongs to.
DvNameOf {
  s.E () = (NONE);
  s.E ((ST s.E (e.N) s.J t.Sent) e.Rest) = (SOME (e.N));
  s.E (e.S) e.Rest = <DvNameOf s.E e.Rest>;
}

DriveEntry {
  (e.States) (NONE) = "entry: <none>";
  (e.States) (SOME (e.Fn)) =
    <DriveOut <DvInvoke (CTX (e.States) 0 ()) (e.Fn) ()>>;
}

DriveOut {
  (OK (e.Out) (CTX (e.States) s.Steps (e.Visited))) =
    "steps: " s.Steps <NL>
    "visited: " <DvVis (e.Visited)> <NL>
    "output: " <EmitTerms e.Out>;
  (e.Err) = "drive error";
}

DvVis {
  () = ;
  (s.Id) = "S" s.Id;
  (s.Id e.Rest) = "S" s.Id " -> " <DvVis (e.Rest)>;
}

* ---- contraction

* The context is (CTX <states> <steps> <visited>). It is threaded through every
* evaluation, including the ones that fail, because the step count is a property
* of the whole walk and not of the successful path through it.
DvInvoke {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) = <DvInvokeGo (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) <DvProut (e.Fn)>>;
}

DvInvokeGo {
  (CTX (e.States) s.Steps (e.Visited)) (e.Fn) (e.In) '1' =
    (OK (e.In) (CTX (e.States) <Add s.Steps 1> (e.Visited)));
  (CTX (e.States) s.Steps (e.Visited)) (e.Fn) (e.In), <Compare s.Steps 10000> : '-' =
    <DvSent (CTX (e.States) <Add s.Steps 1> (e.Visited)) (e.Fn) (e.In) e.States>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) = (ERR (STEPLIMIT));
}

DvProut {
  (e.Fn) = <SameChars (<Upper e.Fn>) ('PROUT')>;
}

* States are tried in order and the first whose pattern and conditions hold
* wins. The state list is spread, so "no states left" is an arity, not a
* sentinel.
DvSent {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) = <DvNoSent (e.Fn)>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (ST s.Id (e.N) s.J t.Sent) e.Rest,
    <SameChars (<Upper e.N>) (<Upper e.Fn>)> : '1' =
      <DvState (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Id t.Sent e.Rest>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (ST s.Id (e.N) s.J t.Sent) e.Rest =
      <DvSent (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) e.Rest>;
}

DvNoSent {
  (e.Fn) = (ERR (NOSENTENCE));
}

DvState {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Id (SENT (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Res)) e.Rest =
    <DvState2 <DvMatch (e.Pat) (e.In) ()> (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Id (e.Conds) (e.Res) e.Rest>;
}

DvState2 {
  (OK (e.B)) (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Id (e.Conds) (e.Res) e.Rest =
    <DvState3 <DvConds (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Conds)> (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Id (e.Res) e.Rest>;
  (NO) (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Id (e.Conds) (e.Res) e.Rest =
    <DvSent (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) e.Rest>;
}

* The state is recorded BEFORE its result is instantiated, which is why a
* recursive function's own state appears once per turn.
DvState3 {
  (OK (e.B2) (CTX (e.States) s.Steps (e.Visited))) (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Id (e.Res) e.Rest =
    <DvInst (CTX (e.States) s.Steps (e.Visited s.Id)) (e.B2) (e.Res)>;
  (NO (e.Ctx1)) (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Id (e.Res) e.Rest =
    <DvSent (e.Ctx1) (e.Fn) (e.In) e.Rest>;
}

* ---- conditions

* (OK <bindings> <ctx>) or (NO <ctx>). A condition's expression is instantiated
* first -- and may invoke -- and only then is its pattern matched against the
* value.
DvConds {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) () = (OK (e.B) (e.Ctx));
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) ((COND (e.Expr) ((BLK (e.Arg) e.Sents))) e.More) =
    <DvCondsBlk (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Arg) (e.Sents) (e.More)>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) ((COND (e.Expr) (e.CPat)) e.More) =
    <DvCondsVal <DvInst (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Expr)> (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.CPat) (e.More)>;
}

DvCondsVal {
  (OK (e.Val) (e.Ctx1)) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.CPat) (e.More) =
    <DvCondsPat <DvMatch (e.CPat) (e.Val) (e.B)> (e.Ctx1) (e.B) (e.More)>;
  (e.Err) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.CPat) (e.More) = (NO (e.Ctx));
}

DvCondsPat {
  (OK (e.B2)) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.More) = <DvConds (e.Ctx) (e.B2) (e.More)>;
  (NO) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.More) = (NO (e.Ctx));
}

DvCondsBlk {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Arg) (e.Sents) (e.More) =
    <DvCondsBlk2 <DvInst (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Arg)> (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Sents) (e.More)>;
}

DvCondsBlk2 {
  (OK (e.Val) (e.Ctx1)) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Sents) (e.More) =
    <DvCondsBlk3 <DvBlkTest (e.Ctx1) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Sents)> (e.B) (e.More)>;
  (e.Err) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Sents) (e.More) = (NO (e.Ctx));
}

DvCondsBlk3 {
  (OK (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.More) = <DvConds (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.More)>;
  (NO (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.More) = (NO (e.Ctx));
}

* A block in condition position is an anonymous function applied to the value:
* the condition holds exactly when one of the block's sentences matches. The
* bindings a block makes stay inside it, which is why the block is tested
* against the enclosing bindings but never writes back to them.
DvBlkTest {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Sents) = <DvBlkTestL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Sents)>;
}

DvBlkTestL {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) () = (NO (e.Ctx));
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) ((SENT (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Res)) e.More) =
    <DvBlkTest2 <DvMatch (e.Pat) (e.Val) (e.B)> (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Conds) (e.More)>;
}

DvBlkTest2 {
  (OK (e.B2)) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Conds) (e.More) =
    <DvBlkTest3 <DvConds (e.Ctx) (e.B2) (e.Conds)> (e.B) (e.Val) (e.More)>;
  (NO) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Conds) (e.More) =
    <DvBlkTestL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.More)>;
}

DvBlkTest3 {
  (OK (e.B3) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.More) = (OK (e.Ctx));
  (NO (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.More) = <DvBlkTestL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.More)>;
}

* ---- instantiation

* (OK <terms> <ctx>) or an error. A call is instantiated by instantiating its
* arguments, invoking, and splicing the result in place; a variable by its
* binding; a bracket by recursing inside it.
DvInst {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Terms) = <DvInstL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Terms)>;
}

DvInstL {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) () = (OK () (e.Ctx));
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (t.T e.Rest) = <DvInstOne (e.Ctx) (e.B) t.T (e.Rest)>;
}

DvInstOne {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (VAR s.K e.I) (e.Rest) =
    <DvInstVar <DvLookup (e.B) (<Canon e.I>)> (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Rest)>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (BR e.Inner) (e.Rest) = <DvInstBr (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Inner) (e.Rest)>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (CALL (ID e.N) e.Args) (e.Rest) =
    <DvInstCall (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.N) (e.Args) (e.Rest)>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (BLK (e.Arg) e.Sents) (e.Rest) =
    <DvInstBlk (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Arg) (e.Sents) (e.Rest)>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) t.T (e.Rest) = <DvInstJoin (t.T) <DvInstL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Rest)>>;
}

DvInstVar {
  (SOME (e.V)) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Rest) = <DvInstL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.V e.Rest)>;
  (NONE) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Rest) = (ERR (UNBOUND));
}

DvInstBr {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Inner) (e.Rest) =
    <DvInstBr2 <DvInst (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Inner)> (e.B) (e.Rest)>;
}

DvInstBr2 {
  (OK (e.In) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Rest) =
    <DvInstJoin ((BR e.In)) <DvInstL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Rest)>>;
  (e.Err) (e.B) (e.Rest) = (e.Err);
}

DvInstCall {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.N) (e.Args) (e.Rest) =
    <DvInstCall2 <DvInst (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Args)> (e.B) (e.N) (e.Rest)>;
}

DvInstCall2 {
  (OK (e.ArgsOut) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.N) (e.Rest) =
    <DvInstCall3 <DvInvoke (e.Ctx) (e.N) (e.ArgsOut)> (e.B) (e.Rest)>;
  (e.Err) (e.B) (e.N) (e.Rest) = (e.Err);
}

DvInstCall3 {
  (OK (e.Out) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Rest) =
    <DvInstJoin (e.Out) <DvInstL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Rest)>>;
  (e.Err) (e.B) (e.Rest) = (e.Err);
}

DvInstBlk {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Arg) (e.Sents) (e.Rest) =
    <DvInstBlk2 <DvInst (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Arg)> (e.B) (e.Sents) (e.Rest)>;
}

DvInstBlk2 {
  (OK (e.Val) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Sents) (e.Rest) =
    <DvInstBlk3 <DvBlkRun (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Sents)> (e.B) (e.Rest)>;
  (e.Err) (e.B) (e.Sents) (e.Rest) = (e.Err);
}

DvInstBlk3 {
  (OK (e.Out) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Rest) =
    <DvInstJoin (e.Out) <DvInstL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Rest)>>;
  (e.Err) (e.B) (e.Rest) = (e.Err);
}

* A block in RESULT position is different from one in condition position: this
* one's matched sentence result is instantiated and its value returned.
DvBlkRun {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Sents) = <DvBlkRunL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Sents)>;
}

DvBlkRunL {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) () = (ERR (BLOCK));
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) ((SENT (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Res)) e.More) =
    <DvBlkRun2 <DvMatch (e.Pat) (e.Val) (e.B)> (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.More)>;
}

DvBlkRun2 {
  (OK (e.B2)) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.More) =
    <DvBlkRun3 <DvConds (e.Ctx) (e.B2) (e.Conds)> (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Res) (e.More)>;
  (NO) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.More) =
    <DvBlkRunL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.More)>;
}

DvBlkRun3 {
  (OK (e.B3) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Res) (e.More) = <DvInst (e.Ctx) (e.B3) (e.Res)>;
  (NO (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Res) (e.More) = <DvBlkRunL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.More)>;
}

DvInstJoin {
  (e.Emit) (OK (e.Out) (e.Ctx)) = (OK (e.Emit e.Out) (e.Ctx));
  (e.Emit) (e.Err) = (e.Err);
}

* ---- matching, the ground case

* match_ground_pattern, with the same backtracking and the same search order:
* an e-variable tries the LONGEST prefix first, an s- or t-variable has exactly
* one candidate and it must be a single term. (e.X) (e.Y) is where that order
* shows, and it is the Rust matcher's order, not the runtime's.
DvMatch {
  (e.Pat) (e.In) (e.B) = <DvMatchL <DvFirst (e.Pat)> (e.In) (e.B)>;
}

DvMatchL {
  (NONE) (e.In) (e.B) = <DvMatchEnd (e.In) (e.B)>;
  (SOME t.P (e.PRest)) (e.In) (e.B) = <DvMatchTerm t.P (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B)>;
}

DvMatchEnd {
  () (e.B) = (OK (e.B));
  t.X (e.B) = (NO);
}

DvMatchTerm {
  (VAR s.K e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) = <DvMatchVar s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B)>;
  t.P (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) = <DvMatchLit t.P (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B)>;
}

DvMatchLit {
  t.P (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) = <DvMatchLit2 <DvFirst (e.In)> t.P (e.PRest) (e.B)>;
}

DvMatchLit2 {
  (NONE) t.P (e.PRest) (e.B) = (NO);
  (SOME t.Q (e.IRest)) t.P (e.PRest) (e.B) =
    <DvMatchLit3 <DvGround t.P t.Q> (e.PRest) (e.IRest) (e.B)>;
}

DvMatchLit3 {
  '1' (e.PRest) (e.IRest) (e.B) = <DvMatch (e.PRest) (e.IRest) (e.B)>;
  (e.PRest) (e.IRest) (e.B) = (NO);
}

DvMatchVar {
  s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) =
    <DvVarTryL <DvVarCands s.K (e.In)> s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B)>;
}

* The candidate list is a BRACKET holding split brackets, not a spread list:
* a spread list handed to a function whose base case is "no candidates" cannot
* tell "empty" from "absent", and the first version of this wrapped it once too
* often so the head pattern never matched at all.
DvVarCands {
  'e' (e.In) = <DvRev (<DvSplits (e.In)>)>;
  's' (e.In) = <DvOneCand (e.In)>;
  't' (e.In) = <DvOneCand (e.In)>;
}

* Every prefix/suffix split, shortest prefix first.
DvSplits {
  (e.In) = <DvSplits2 () (e.In)>;
}

DvSplits2 {
  (e.Pre) (e.Suf) = ((e.Pre) (e.Suf)) <DvSplits3 (e.Pre) (e.Suf)>;
}

DvSplits3 {
  (e.Pre) () = ;
  (e.Pre) (t.X e.Rest) = <DvSplits2 (e.Pre t.X) (e.Rest)>;
}

DvRev {
  (e.Splits) = <DvRevL (e.Splits) ()>;
}

* The accumulator is passed as a BRACKET and bound by its contents, so the
* recursive construction `(t.X e.Acc)` prepends a term to the list instead of
* appending a bracket to it. Binding it as a bracket and constructing with it
* adds the bracket itself as a term, which is how the first version turned the
* empty tail into a stray `()` that no later pattern matched.
DvRevL {
  () (e.Acc) = (e.Acc);
  (t.X e.Rest) (e.Acc) = <DvRevL (e.Rest) (t.X e.Acc)>;
}

DvOneCand {
  () = ();
  (t.X e.Rest) = (((t.X) (e.Rest)));
}

DvVarTryL {
  () s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) = (NO);
  (((e.Pre) (e.Suf)) e.More) s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) =
    <DvVarCand <DvVarOk s.K (e.Pre)> s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) (e.Pre) (e.Suf) (e.More)>;
}

DvVarOk {
  'e' (e.Pre) = '1';
  't' (e.Pre) = <DvSingle (e.Pre)>;
  's' (e.Pre) = <DvSymbol (e.Pre)>;
}

* An `s.` binds any symbol -- a character, a number or an identifier -- but
* never a bracket. A `t.` binds any single term, bracket included (reference
* 1.3).
DvSingle {
  (t.X) = '1';
  (e.A) = ;
}

DvSymbol {
  ((SYM s.C)) = '1';
  ((ID e.N)) = '1';
  ((NUM e.D)) = '1';
  (e.A) = ;
}

DvVarCand {
  '1' s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) (e.Pre) (e.Suf) (e.More) =
    <DvVarBind <DvLookup (e.B) (<Canon e.I>)> s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) (e.Pre) (e.Suf) (e.More)>;
  s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) (e.Pre) (e.Suf) (e.More) =
    <DvVarTryL (e.More) s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B)>;
}

DvVarBind {
  (NONE) s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) (e.Pre) (e.Suf) (e.More) =
    <DvVarAccept <DvMatch (e.PRest) (e.Suf) (e.B (VE (<Canon e.I>) (e.Pre)))>
      s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) (e.More)>;
  (SOME (e.Old)) s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) (e.Pre) (e.Suf) (e.More) =
    <DvVarRep <SameChars (e.Old) (e.Pre)> s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) (e.Pre) (e.Suf) (e.More)>;
}

* A repeated variable must bind the same terms everywhere it occurs, so the
* existing binding is reused rather than replaced.
DvVarRep {
  '1' s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) (e.Pre) (e.Suf) (e.More) =
    <DvVarAccept <DvMatch (e.PRest) (e.Suf) (e.B)> s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) (e.More)>;
  s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) (e.Pre) (e.Suf) (e.More) =
    <DvVarTryL (e.More) s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B)>;
}

DvVarAccept {
  (OK (e.B2)) s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) (e.More) = (OK (e.B2));
  (NO) s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B) (e.More) =
    <DvVarTryL (e.More) s.K (e.I) (e.PRest) (e.In) (e.B)>;
}

DvGround {
  (SYM s.A) (SYM s.B) = <SameChars (s.A) (s.B)>;
  (ID e.A) (ID e.B) = <SameChars (<Upper e.A>) (<Upper e.B>)>;
  (NUM e.A) (NUM e.B) = <SameChars (e.A) (e.B)>;
  (BR e.A) (BR e.B) = <DvMatched <DvMatch (e.A) (e.B) ()>>;
  t.X t.Y = ;
}

DvMatched {
  (OK (e.B)) = '1';
  (NO) = ;
}

* ---- small list utilities

DvFirst {
  () = (NONE);
  (t.X e.Rest) = (SOME t.X (e.Rest));
}

DvLookup {
  (e.B) (e.Key) = <DvLookupL (e.B) (e.Key)>;
}

DvLookupL {
  () (e.Key) = (NONE);
  ((VE (e.K) (e.V)) e.Rest) (e.Key), <SameChars (e.K) (e.Key)> : '1' = (SOME (e.V));
  (t.F e.Rest) (e.Key) = <DvLookupL (e.Rest) (e.Key)>;
}

* ---------------------------------------------------------- symbolic drive
*
* Turchin 1980 section 4.2: driving a configuration whose argument is not
* ground. This reproduces refal-core's `drive_symbolic_with_strategy`
* (crates/refal-core/src/lib.rs:693) on top of the Graph stage above, exactly as
* DRIVE reproduced `drive_ground`. It is the stage that makes the driver a
* *compiler* rather than a reporter: the ground driver can only contract a
* closed configuration, so it can never compile a function whose input is
* unknown, while this one partitions an unknown argument into the three
* exhaustive, pairwise disjoint cases -- `[]`, `s.H e.T`, `(e.B) e.T` -- drives
* each branch, and emits a generated function whose sentences are those
* branches.
*
* The three pieces the ground driver does not have:
*
*  1. A three-valued matcher. `match_symbolic_pattern` answers Yes, No or
*     Unknown, and Unknown means the answer depends on information driving does
*     not have. Guessing a branch there is what makes a driver unsound.
*  2. Folding against the active path. A configuration that recurs while it is
*     still being expanded is a cycle, and the generated function is what it
*     folds to.
*  3. The whistle. A configuration that grows relative to one already seen at
*     the same state cannot be driven further; the residue keeps a call.
*
* Four conventions differ from the ground driver, and each is load bearing.
*
*  - Every list-valued field of the context is stored BRACKETED, and every
*    walker takes its list as one bracketed argument. A walker that takes a list
*    spread cannot tell "empty" from "absent" once the list is handed on, which
*    is the trap the seed graph's firsts table already paid for.
*  - Every multi-symbol string stored inside a structure is bracketed, because
*    `(e.X)` in a pattern matches a bracket and a bare identifier is not one.
*  - The context is threaded through *every* result, including the failures,
*    because invocation is what appends configurations and transitions, and
*    invocation happens inside condition matching and inside argument
*    instantiation as well as at the top.
*  - The verdict of an invocation is `(RED <terms> <ctx>)`, `(RES <ctx>)` or
*    `(FAILS <ctx>)`; the verdict of a condition is `(MY <bindings> <ctx>)`,
*    `(MN <ctx>)` or `(MU <ctx>)`. Keeping them distinct from the ground
*    driver's `(OK ...)`/`(NO)` is deliberate: the symbolic driver threads a
*    context through both, and overloading one tag for two protocols is how a
*    stage silently returns the wrong thing.
*
* `record_whistle` accumulates whistle states, whistle inputs and whistle
* events. None of the three is printed by `refal drive-symbolic`, so
* `DsWhistle` is a no-op here and says so rather than pretending.
*
* Blocks are not walked by the configuration transformers below
* (`DsCanonical`, `DsRename`, `DsVarPairs`, `DsEmbeds`), and that is a
* consequence rather than an omission: a configuration's argument is either the
* entry's single `e.`-variable, a branch built by `DsReplace` out of variables
* and brackets, or the *instantiated* argument of a call -- and
* `DsInstantiate` evaluates a block rather than leaving it in place. So no
* configuration, and therefore no `visited_inputs`, `active_path` or
* `completed` entry, ever holds a block. `DsContainsSym` does walk blocks,
* because it is applied to source patterns and results, where they do occur.

* ---- the stage entry
*
* Modes: SYM is the default report, SYMCFG adds the configuration list and the
* transitions, SYMNEI adds the neighborhoods, SYMBOTH adds both, and SYMI is
* the interpretive strategy -- Turchin's own loop-back rule (1988 section 4),
* off by default because the compilative end is what a metasystem transition
* needs.

DriveSym {
  (e.Mode) e.Source =
    <DsRun (e.Mode) <Parse <Lex (<Chr 10>) <StripCR (<Chr 13>) e.Source>>>>;
}

DsRun {
  (e.Mode) (PROG e.Items) =
    <DsRun2 (e.Mode) <DsStrategy (e.Mode)> <CleanG <BuildG e.Items>>>;
}

DsStrategy {
  (SYMI) = 'I';
  (e.Mode) = 'C';
}

DsRun2 {
  (e.Mode) s.Strat (CG () (e.States) (e.Trans)) = "symbolic drive error: no entry";
  (e.Mode) s.Strat (CG (s.E) (e.States) (e.Trans)) =
    <DsTop (e.Mode) s.Strat (e.States) s.E <DvNameOf s.E (e.States)>>;
}

DsTop {
  (e.Mode) s.Strat (e.States) s.E (NONE) = "symbolic drive error: no entry";
  (e.Mode) s.Strat (e.States) s.E (SOME (e.Fn)) =
    <DsOut (e.Mode) (e.Fn)
      <DsInvoke (SX s.E s.Strat (e.States) 0 () () () () () () () (NONE) 0 ())
                (e.Fn) ((VAR 'e' 'Input'))>>;
}

DsOut {
  (e.Mode) (e.Fn) (RED (e.Out) (e.Ctx)) =
    <DsOut2 (e.Mode) (e.Out) <DsRewire <DsLoop (e.Ctx)>>>;
  (e.Mode) (e.Fn) (RES (e.Ctx)) =
    <DsOut2 (e.Mode) <DsCallSeq (e.Fn) ((VAR 'e' 'Input'))> <DsRewire <DsLoop (e.Ctx)>>>;
  (e.Mode) (e.Fn) (FAILS (e.Ctx)) =
    <DsOut2 (e.Mode) <DsCallSeq (e.Fn) ((VAR 'e' 'Input'))> <DsRewire <DsLoop (e.Ctx)>>>;
  (e.Mode) (e.Fn) (ERR) = "symbolic drive error: step limit";
}

DsOut2 {
  (e.Mode) (e.Residual) (LOOP (e.Ctx)) = <DsReport (e.Mode) (e.Residual) (e.Ctx)>;
  (e.Mode) (e.Residual) (ERR) = "symbolic drive error: step limit";
}

* ------------------------------------------------ driven residualization
*
* `refal residualize-driven` is the command that makes the compiler a compiler
* rather than a normaliser. It drives the entry CONFIGURATION -- the entry
* applied to the arguments the program is actually run on -- and emits the
* program the driven graph denotes. The driving half is the Ds* machinery
* above, unchanged. What this section adds is the entry-argument decision, the
* projection of the driven graph back into a program, and the transitive
* retention of every function the residue still calls.
*
* The entry argument is the whole difference between this and
* `refal drive-symbolic`, and it is not a detail. `drive_symbolic` always
* supplies `e.Input`, and a Refal `Go` normally takes nothing: supplying
* `e.Input` to it matches no sentence, drives nothing, and residualises the
* program to itself. Driving the CLOSED configuration is what makes
* `drive -> residualise` mean something for a complete program (1980 4.2).

DsRdRun {
  e.Source =
    <DsRdRun2 <Parse <Lex (<Chr 10>) <StripCR (<Chr 13>) e.Source>>>>;
}

DsRdRun2 {
  (PROG e.Items) = <DsRdRun3 (PROG e.Items) <CleanG <BuildG e.Items>>>;
}

DsRdRun3 {
  (PROG e.Items) (CG () (e.States) (e.Trans)) =
    "driven residualization error: no entry";
  (PROG e.Items) (CG (s.E) (e.States) (e.Trans)) =
    <DsRdTop (PROG e.Items) (e.States) s.E>;
}

DsRdTop {
  (PROG e.Items) (e.States) s.E =
    <DsRdTop2 (PROG e.Items) (e.States) s.E <DvNameOf s.E (e.States)>>;
}

DsRdTop2 {
  (PROG e.Items) (e.States) s.E (NONE) =
    "driven residualization error: no entry";
  (PROG e.Items) (e.States) s.E (SOME (e.Fn)) =
    <DsRdGo (PROG e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.States) s.E <DsRdArgs (e.States) s.E>>;
}

DsRdGo {
  (PROG e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.States) s.E (e.Args) =
    <DsRdOut (PROG e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Args)
      <DsInvoke (SX s.E 'C' (e.States) 0 () () () () () () () (NONE) 0 ())
                (e.Fn) (e.Args)>>;
}

* The arguments the entry configuration is driven with: none when the entry
* state's sentence takes no arguments, `e.Input` otherwise. Both are returned
* bracketed, so `()` and a one-term list are the same shape to the caller.
DsRdArgs {
  (e.States) s.E, <DsEntryClosed (e.States) s.E> : '1' = ();
  (e.States) s.E = ((VAR 'e' 'Input'));
}

DsEntryClosed {
  (e.States) s.E = <DsEntryClosedL (e.States) s.E>;
}

DsEntryClosedL {
  () s.E = ;
  ((ST s.E (e.N) s.J (SENT () (e.C) (e.R))) e.Rest) s.E = '1';
  ((ST s.E (e.N) s.J t.Sent) e.Rest) s.E = ;
  (t.S e.Rest) s.E = <DsEntryClosedL (e.Rest) s.E>;
}

DsRdOut {
  (PROG e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Args) (RED (e.Out) (e.Ctx)) =
    <DsRdEmit (PROG e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Out) <DsRewire <DsLoop (e.Ctx)>>>;
  (PROG e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Args) (RES (e.Ctx)) =
    <DsRdEmit (PROG e.Items) (e.Fn) <DsCallSeq (e.Fn) (e.Args)> <DsRewire <DsLoop (e.Ctx)>>>;
  (PROG e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Args) (FAILS (e.Ctx)) =
    <DsRdEmit (PROG e.Items) (e.Fn) <DsCallSeq (e.Fn) (e.Args)> <DsRewire <DsLoop (e.Ctx)>>>;
  (PROG e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Args) (ERR) =
    "driven residualization error: step limit";
}

DsRdEmit {
  (PROG e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Residual) (LOOP (e.Ctx)) =
    <DsRdReport (e.Ctx)> <Emit <DsRdBuild (e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Residual) (e.Ctx)>>;
  (PROG e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Residual) (ERR) =
    "driven residualization error: step limit";
}

DsRdReport {
  (e.Ctx) =
    "steps: " <DsSteps (e.Ctx)> <NL>
    "visited: " <DsVis (e.Ctx)> <NL>
    "whistles: " <DsWStateLine (e.Ctx)> <NL>
    "generalized: " <DsLen <DsWEvents (e.Ctx)>> <NL>
    "neighborhood-loops: " <DsLoops (e.Ctx)> <NL>
    "generalized-states: " <DsWStateLine (e.Ctx)> <NL>;
}

DsWEvents {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) =
    (e.WE);
}

* Both the `whistles` line and the `generalized-states` line come from the
* event list, not from the distinct state set: the Rust command formats both
* from `whistle_events`, so a state that whistled on two different triples
* appears twice on both lines, and `generalized` is that list's length.
DsWStateLine {
  (e.Ctx) = <DsWStateLineL <DsWEvents (e.Ctx)>>;
}

DsWStateLineL {
  () = ;
  ((WE s.Id t.P t.I)) = "S" s.Id;
  ((WE s.Id t.P t.I) e.Rest) = "S" s.Id ", " <DsWStateLineL (e.Rest)>;
}
* ---- the residue as a program

DsRdBuild {
  (e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Residual) (e.Ctx) =
    <DsRdBuild2 (e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Residual) (e.Ctx)
      <DsRdSelfLoop (e.Fn) (e.Residual)>>;
}

* A residue that is exactly `<Entry e.X>` is the program itself: driving
* learned nothing, and re-emitting it would rename the entry's argument for no
* reason. The Rust residualizer short-circuits on the same shape, and it has
* to: without it every program whose entry takes an expression variable would
* come back as a different program that happens to mean the same thing.
DsRdSelfLoop {
  (e.Fn) ((CALL (ID e.Fn) (VAR 'e' e.N))) = '1';
  (e.Fn) (e.R) = ;
}

* The residue has to accept whatever the entry accepts. A `Go { = ...; }`
* takes no arguments, and giving it an `e.Input` pattern would widen the
* program's interface -- the residue would then answer calls the original
* could not, and the two programs would no longer be interchangeable.
DsRdBuild2 {
  (e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Residual) (e.Ctx) '1' = (PROG e.Items);
  (e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Residual) (e.Ctx) =
    (PROG
      <DsRdDecls e.Items>
      (FUN (Ident e.Fn) (VIS ENTRY) <DsRdEntry (e.Items) (e.Residual)>)
      <DsRdSplits (e.Ctx)>
      <DsRdRetain (e.Items) (e.Fn) <DsRdReach (e.Residual) (e.Ctx)>
                  <DsRdSplitNames (e.Ctx)>>);
}

DsRdEntry {
  (e.Items) (e.Residual), <DsRdClosed (e.Items)> : '1' = (SENT () () (e.Residual));
  (e.Items) (e.Residual) = (SENT ((VAR 'e' 'Input')) () (e.Residual));
}

* Whether the entry FUNCTION's first sentence takes no arguments. The driving
* decision reads the entry STATE's pattern and this reads the entry function's
* first sentence, which is the state's own sentence; the two agree, and the
* Rust residualizer reads the function here and the graph there.
DsRdClosed {
  (e.Items) = <DsRdClosedL e.Items>;
}

DsRdClosedL {
  = ;
  (FUN (Ident e.N) (VIS ENTRY) (SENT () (e.C) (e.R)) e.More) e.Rest = '1';
  (FUN (Ident e.N) (VIS ENTRY) t.S e.More) e.Rest = ;
  t.Item e.Rest = <DsRdClosedL e.Rest>;
}

DsRdDecls {
  = ;
  (EXT e.Names) e.Rest = (EXT e.Names) <DsRdDecls e.Rest>;
  t.Item e.Rest = <DsRdDecls e.Rest>;
}

* A case split is part of the residue, not scaffolding around it: the entry
* calls `Split1`, so `Split1` has to be defined.
DsRdSplits {
  (e.Ctx) = <DsRdSplitsL <DsSplits (e.Ctx)>>;
}

DsRdSplitsL {
  () = ;
  ((SP (e.Name) (e.F) (e.Can) (e.Sents)) e.Rest) =
    (FUN (Ident e.Name) (VIS LOCAL) e.Sents) <DsRdSplitsL (e.Rest)>;
}

DsRdSplitNames {
  (e.Ctx) = (<DsRdSplitNamesL <DsSplits (e.Ctx)>>);
}

DsRdSplitNamesL {
  () = ;
  ((SP (e.Name) (e.F) (e.Can) (e.Sents)) e.Rest) =
    (e.Name) <DsRdSplitNamesL (e.Rest)>;
}

* Driving stops at a call it cannot decide and leaves it in the residue. The
* residue is a program, so every user function it still calls has to come with
* it -- otherwise the residue does not check, and a residualizer that emits a
* program the compiler rejects has emitted nothing.
DsRdReach {
  (e.Residual) (e.Ctx) = (<DsRdCalls (e.Residual)> <DsRdSplitCalls <DsSplits (e.Ctx)>>);
}

DsRdSplitCalls {
  () = ;
  ((SP (e.Name) (e.F) (e.Can) (e.Sents)) e.Rest) =
    <DsRdSentCalls (e.Sents)> <DsRdSplitCalls (e.Rest)>;
}

* Split sentences carry no conditions, which is why this is not the same
* function the retention pass uses: the two collect from the same terms the
* Rust residualizer collects from, and it collects from the pattern and the
* result only here.
DsRdSentCalls {
  () = ;
  ((SENT (e.P) (e.C) (e.R)) e.Rest) =
    <DsRdCalls (e.P)> <DsRdCalls (e.R)> <DsRdSentCalls (e.Rest)>;
}

* Names come out BRACKETED, one per call. A name is a character sequence, not
* one symbol, so a spread list of names could not be split back into names --
* `(e.N e.Rest)` would take the empty prefix and never consume the list. That
* is the same shape of trap as the two adjacent expression variables in the
* matcher, one level up.
DsRdCalls {
  (e.Terms) = <DsRdCallsL (e.Terms)>;
}

DsRdCallsL {
  () = ;
  ((CALL (ID e.N) e.Args) e.Rest) = (N e.N) <DsRdCallsL (e.Args)> <DsRdCallsL (e.Rest)>;
  ((BR e.Inner) e.Rest) = <DsRdCallsL (e.Inner)> <DsRdCallsL (e.Rest)>;
  ((BLK (e.Arg) e.Sents) e.Rest) =
    <DsRdCallsL (e.Arg)> <DsRdBlkSents (e.Sents)> <DsRdCallsL (e.Rest)>;
  (t.X e.Rest) = <DsRdCallsL (e.Rest)>;
}

DsRdBlkSents {
  () = ;
  ((SENT (e.P) (e.C) (e.R)) e.Rest) =
    <DsRdCallsL (e.P)> <DsRdConds (e.C)> <DsRdCallsL (e.R)> <DsRdBlkSents (e.Rest)>;
}

DsRdConds {
  () = ;
  ((COND (e.Expr) (e.CPat)) e.Rest) =
    <DsRdCallsL (e.Expr)> <DsRdCallsL (e.CPat)> <DsRdConds (e.Rest)>;
}

DsRdRetain {
  (e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Reachable) (e.Generated) =
    <DsRdDropGen (e.Generated) (<DsRdRetainRaw (e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Reachable)>)>;
}

DsRdRetainRaw {
  (e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Reachable), <DsRdHasMu (e.Reachable)> : '1' =
    <DsRdAllBut (e.Items) (e.Fn)>;
  (e.Items) (e.Fn) (e.Reachable) =
    <DsRdBfs (e.Items) (e.Reachable) () (<DsRdUpper (e.Fn)>)>;
}

* `Mu` dispatches on a function name carried as *data*, so walking call terms
* cannot see what it will call. When the residue still dispatches dynamically
* the only sound residue keeps every definition the original had -- a residue
* that drops `Echo` is a program that fails at run time where the original
* succeeded.
DsRdBfs {
  (e.Items) () (e.Acc) (e.Seen) = e.Acc;
  (e.Items) ((N e.Name) e.Rest) (e.Acc) (e.Seen),
    <DsRdSeenQ (e.Seen) (e.Name)> : '1' = <DsRdBfs (e.Items) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) (e.Seen)>;
  (e.Items) ((N e.Name) e.Rest) (e.Acc) (e.Seen),
    <DsRdFindFun (e.Items) (e.Name)> : (SOME ((FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents))) =
      <DsRdBfs (e.Items) (e.Rest <DsRdFunCalls (e.Sents)>)
               (e.Acc (FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents))
               (e.Seen (<DsRdUpper (e.Name)>))>;
  (e.Items) ((N e.Name) e.Rest) (e.Acc) (e.Seen) =
    <DsRdBfs (e.Items) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) (e.Seen)>;
}

* The order is the Rust pass's order -- pattern, result, then each condition's
* result and pattern -- because the discovery order of the work list is the
* emission order of the retained definitions.
DsRdFunCalls {
  (e.Sents) = <DsRdFullSents (e.Sents)>;
}

DsRdFullSents {
  () = ;
  ((SENT (e.P) (e.C) (e.R)) e.Rest) =
    <DsRdCalls (e.P)> <DsRdCalls (e.R)> <DsRdConds (e.C)> <DsRdFullSents (e.Rest)>;
}

DsRdSeenQ {
  (e.Seen) (e.Name) = <DsRdSeenL (e.Seen) (<DsRdUpper (e.Name)>)>;
}

DsRdSeenL {
  () (e.U) = ;
  ((e.S) e.Rest) (e.U), <SameChars (e.S) (e.U)> : '1' = '1';
  (t.X e.Rest) (e.U) = <DsRdSeenL (e.Rest) (e.U)>;
}

DsRdFindFun {
  (e.Items) (e.Name) = <DsRdFindFunL (<DsRdUpper (e.Name)>) e.Items>;
}

DsRdFindFunL {
  (e.U) = (NONE);
  (e.U) (FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents) e.Rest,
    <SameChars (<Upper e.N>) (e.U)> : '1' =
      (SOME ((FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents)));
  (e.U) t.Item e.Rest = <DsRdFindFunL (e.U) e.Rest>;
}

DsRdHasMu {
  (e.L) = <DsRdHasMuL (e.L)>;
}

DsRdHasMuL {
  () = ;
  ((N e.Name) e.Rest), <SameChars (<Upper e.Name>) ('MU')> : '1' = '1';
  (t.X e.Rest) = <DsRdHasMuL (e.Rest)>;
}

DsRdAllBut {
  (e.Items) (e.Fn) = <DsRdAllButL (<DsRdUpper (e.Fn)>) e.Items>;
}

DsRdAllButL {
  (e.U) = ;
  (e.U) (FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents) e.Rest,
    <SameChars (<Upper e.N>) (e.U)> : '1' = <DsRdAllButL (e.U) e.Rest>;
  (e.U) (FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents) e.Rest =
    (FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents) <DsRdAllButL (e.U) e.Rest>;
  (e.U) t.Item e.Rest = <DsRdAllButL (e.U) e.Rest>;
}

* The generated split functions are already emitted; carrying the source's
* copy of one as well would be a duplicate definition.
DsRdDropGen {
  (e.Generated) (e.Funs) = <DsRdDropGenL (e.Funs) (e.Generated)>;
}

DsRdDropGenL {
  () (e.G) = ;
  ((FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents) e.Rest) (e.G),
    <DsRdNameIn (e.G) (e.N)> : '1' = <DsRdDropGenL (e.Rest) (e.G)>;
  ((FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents) e.Rest) (e.G) =
    (FUN (Ident e.N) (e.V) e.Sents) <DsRdDropGenL (e.Rest) (e.G)>;
  (t.X e.Rest) (e.G) = <DsRdDropGenL (e.Rest) (e.G)>;
}

DsRdNameIn {
  () (e.N) = ;
  ((e.S) e.Rest) (e.N), <SameChars (<Upper e.S>) (<Upper e.N>)> : '1' = '1';
  (t.X e.Rest) (e.N) = <DsRdNameIn (e.Rest) (e.N)>;
}

DsRdUpper {
  (e.N) = <Upper e.N>;
}

* The CLI prints steps, visited, neighborhood-loops, then whichever optional
* sections were asked for, then the residual. The trailing space in
* `C<id>: S<state> <input>` is not cosmetic -- it is what the Rust formatter
* emits for an empty argument, and a differential that ignores it is not a
* differential.
DsReport {
  (e.Mode) (e.Residual) (e.Ctx) =
    "steps: " <DsSteps (e.Ctx)> <NL>
    "visited: " <DsVis (e.Ctx)> <NL>
    "neighborhood-loops: " <DsLoops (e.Ctx)> <NL>
    <DsOptional (e.Mode) (e.Ctx)>
    "residual: " <EmitTerms e.Residual>;
}

DsOptional {
  (SYMNEI) (e.Ctx) = <DsNeighLines (e.Ctx)>;
  (SYMCFG) (e.Ctx) = <DsCfgLines (e.Ctx)>;
  (SYMBOTH) (e.Ctx) = <DsNeighLines (e.Ctx)> <DsCfgLines (e.Ctx)>;
  (e.Mode) (e.Ctx) = ;
}

DsCfgLines {
  (e.Ctx) =
    "configurations: " <DsLen <DsConfs (e.Ctx)>> <NL>
    <DsCfgLinesL <DsConfs (e.Ctx)>>
    "configuration-transitions: " <DsLen <DsConfsTr (e.Ctx)>> <NL>
    <DsTrLinesL <DsConfsTr (e.Ctx)>>;
}

DsCfgLinesL {
  () = ;
  ((CF s.Id s.St (e.In)) e.Rest) =
    "C" s.Id ": S" s.St " " <EmitTerms e.In> <NL> <DsCfgLinesL (e.Rest)>;
}

DsTrLinesL {
  () = ;
  ((CFT s.From (e.Callee) (e.In) (NONE)) e.Rest) =
    "C" s.From " -" e.Callee " " <EmitTerms e.In> "-> residual" <NL> <DsTrLinesL (e.Rest)>;
  ((CFT s.From (e.Callee) (e.In) (SOME s.To)) e.Rest) =
    "C" s.From " -" e.Callee " " <EmitTerms e.In> "-> C" s.To <NL> <DsTrLinesL (e.Rest)>;
}

DsNeighLines {
  (e.Ctx) = <DsNeighLinesL <DsConfs (e.Ctx)>>;
}

DsNeighLinesL {
  () = ;
  ((CF s.Id s.St (e.In)) e.Rest) =
    "N" s.Id ": " <EmitTerms e.In> " [" <EmitTerms <DsNeighOf (e.In)>> "]" <NL>
    <DsNeighLinesL (e.Rest)>;
}

DsVis {
  (e.Ctx) = <DsVisL <DsVisited (e.Ctx)>>;
}

DsVisL {
  () = ;
  (s.Id) = "S" s.Id;
  (s.Id e.Rest) = "S" s.Id " -> " <DsVisL (e.Rest)>;
}

* ---- the context
*
* (SX <entry> <strategy> <states> <steps> <visited> <visited-inputs>
*     <active-path> <splits> <completed> <configurations> <transitions>
*     <active-configuration> <neighborhood-loops> <whistle-events>)
DsSteps {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) = s.S;
}

DsStates {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) = (e.St);
}

DsVisited {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) = (e.V);
}

DsVisitedIn {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) = (e.VI);
}

DsActivePath {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) = (e.AP);
}

DsSplits {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) = (e.SP);
}

DsCompletedList {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) = (e.CP);
}

DsConfs {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) = (e.CF);
}

DsConfsTr {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) = (e.CT);
}

DsActive {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) = t.A;
}

DsLoops {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) = s.N;
}

DsEntry {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) = s.E;
}

DsInterpretive {
  (SX s.E 'I' (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) = '1';
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) = ;
}

DsSetActive {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.Old s.N (e.WE)) t.A =
    (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE));
}

DsBump {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) =
    (SX s.E s.T (e.St) <Add s.S 1> (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE));
}

DsBumpLoops {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) =
    (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A <Add s.N 1> (e.WE));
}

DsPushVisited {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) s.Id,
    <Member s.Id (e.V)> : '1' =
      (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE));
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) s.Id =
    (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V s.Id) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE));
}

DsPushVisitedIn {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE))
      s.Id (e.In) =
    (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI (VI s.Id (e.In))) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT)
        t.A s.N (e.WE));
}

DsPushPath {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE))
      (e.Fn) (e.Can) (e.In) t.Split =
    (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI)
        (e.AP (AP (e.Fn) (e.Can) (e.In) <DsNeigh (e.In)> t.Split))
        (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE));
}

DsPop {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)) =
    (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (<DsDropLast (e.AP)>) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT)
        t.A s.N (e.WE));
}

DsDropLast {
  () = ;
  (t.X) = ;
  (t.X e.Rest) = t.X <DsDropLast (e.Rest)>;
}

DsPushCompleted {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE))
      s.Id (e.Can) (e.In) (e.Out) =
    (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP)
        (e.CP (CP s.Id (e.Can) (e.In) (e.Out))) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE));
}

* ---- invocation

DsInvoke {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) = <DsInvoke0 (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In)>;
}

DsInvoke0 {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In), <DsSteps (e.Ctx)> : s.S, <Compare s.S 10000> : '-' =
    <DsScan <DsBump <DsRecCall (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In)>>
            (e.Fn) (e.In) ('0' '0') <DsStates (e.Ctx)>>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) = (ERR);
}

DsScan {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) () =
    <DsUndecided (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags)>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) ((ST s.Id (e.N) s.J t.Sent) e.Rest),
    <SameChars (<Upper e.N>) (<Upper e.Fn>)> : '1' =
      <DsTryState (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id t.Sent (e.Rest)>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) (t.S e.Rest) =
      <DsScan (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) (e.Rest)>;
}

DsTryState {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (SENT (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Res)) (e.Rest) =
    <DsTryState2 <DsMatchSym (e.Pat) (e.In) ()>
                 (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.Rest)>;
}

DsTryState2 {
  (NO) (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.Rest) =
    <DsScan (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) (e.Rest)>;
  (UNK) (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.Rest) =
    <DsScan (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) <DsFlagUnk (e.Flags)> (e.Rest)>;
  (YES (e.B)) (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.Rest) =
    <DsYes (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.Rest)>;
}

* The state is recorded before its conditions are matched, because a condition's
* expression is instantiated -- and may invoke -- and the calls it makes belong
* to this configuration.
DsYes {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.Rest) =
    <DsYesA (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.Rest)
            <DsRecConf (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In)>>;
}

DsYesA {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.Rest)
      (CID s.Cid (e.Ctx2)) =
    <DsYesB <DsSetActive (e.Ctx2) (SOME s.Cid)>
            (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.Rest)
            s.Cid <DsActive (e.Ctx2)>>;
}

DsYesB {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev =
    <DsYesC <DsMatchConds (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Conds)>
            (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev>;
}

DsYesC {
  (MN (e.Ctx2)) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev =
    <DsScan <DsSetActive (e.Ctx2) t.Prev> (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) (e.Rest)>;
  (MU (e.Ctx2)) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev =
    <DsScan <DsSetActive (e.Ctx2) t.Prev> (e.Fn) (e.In) <DsFlagUnk (e.Flags)> (e.Rest)>;
  (MY (e.B2) (e.Ctx2)) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev =
    <DsYesD (e.Ctx2) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B2) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev>;
  (ERR) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev = (ERR);
}

* A sentence that matched while an earlier one was undecided is not decided by
* matching: the argument has to be partitioned. But first the whistle gets its
* say, because splitting a *growing* argument produces a genuinely new
* configuration every time and never terminates on its own.
DsYesD {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev,
    <DsFlagUnkQ (e.Flags)> : '1' =
      <DsWhistleBlk (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Id t.Prev>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev =
    <DsYesE (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev>;
}

DsWhistleBlk {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Id t.Prev =
    <DsWhistleBlk2 (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Id t.Prev
                       <DsFindEmbedding (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In)>>;
}

DsWhistleBlk2 {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Id t.Prev (SOME (e.PrevIn)) =
    (RES <DsSetActive <DsWhistle (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.PrevIn) (e.In)> t.Prev>);
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Id t.Prev (NONE) =
    <DsBlocked <DsSetActive (e.Ctx) t.Prev> (e.Fn) (e.In)>;
}

DsYesE {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev =
    <DsYesE2 (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev
             <DsCanonical (e.In)>>;
}

DsYesE2 {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev (e.Can) =
    <DsYesE3 (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev
             (e.Can) <DsActiveSplit (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.Can)>>;
}

* The fold. A configuration being built by a case split that recurs is a
* self-call to the generated function, which is what makes the split terminate.
DsYesE3 {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev
      (e.Can) (SOME (e.Name)) =
    (RED <DsCallSeq (e.Name) (e.In)> <DsBump <DsSetActive (e.Ctx) t.Prev>>);
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev
      (e.Can) (NONE) =
    <DsYesF (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev (e.Can)>;
}

* A recurrence on the active path is a cycle and warrants a whistle; a
* recurrence with a configuration that already *finished* is not a cycle at all,
* and reusing its residue is exactly what unwinds an interpreter's recursion
* into straight-line code.
DsYesF {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev (e.Can),
    <DsActiveHas (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.Can)> : '1', <DsAnySymVar (e.In)> : '1' =
      (RES <DsSetActive <DsWhistle (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In) (e.In)> t.Prev>);
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev (e.Can),
    <DsCompleted (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In)> : (SOME (e.Red)) =
      (RED (e.Red) <DsBump <DsSetActive (e.Ctx) t.Prev>>);
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev (e.Can),
    <DsFindEmbedding (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In)> : (SOME (e.PrevIn)) =
      (RES <DsSetActive <DsWhistle (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.PrevIn) (e.In)> t.Prev>);
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev (e.Can),
    <DsInterpretive (e.Ctx)> : '1',
    <DsActiveNeigh (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In)> : (SOME (e.PrevIn)) =
      (RES <DsSetActive <DsBumpLoops <DsWhistle (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.PrevIn) (e.In)>> t.Prev>);
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev (e.Can),
    <DsSeenSame (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In)> : '1' =
      (RES <DsSetActive (e.Ctx) t.Prev>);
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) (e.Rest) s.Cid t.Prev (e.Can) =
    <DsEnter (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) t.Prev (e.Can) s.Cid>;
}

DsEnter {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Id (e.B) (e.Res) t.Prev (e.Can) s.Cid =
    <DsEnterA
      <DsPushPath
        <DsSetActive <DsPushVisited <DsPushVisitedIn (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In)> s.Id> (SOME s.Cid)>
        (e.Fn) (e.Can) (e.In) (NONE)>
      s.Id (e.Can) (e.In) (e.B) (e.Res) t.Prev>;
}

DsEnterA {
  (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.Can) (e.In) (e.B) (e.Res) t.Prev =
    <DsEnterB <DsPopResult <DsInstantiate (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Res)>>
              s.Id (e.Can) (e.In) t.Prev>;
}

DsPopResult {
  (RED (e.Out) (e.Ctx)) = (RED (e.Out) <DsPop (e.Ctx)>);
  (RES (e.Ctx)) = (RES <DsPop (e.Ctx)>);
  (FAILS (e.Ctx)) = (FAILS <DsPop (e.Ctx)>);
  (ERR) = (ERR);
}

DsEnterB {
  (RED (e.Out) (e.Ctx)) s.Id (e.Can) (e.In) t.Prev =
    (RED (e.Out) <DsSetActive <DsPushCompleted (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.Can) (e.In) (e.Out)> t.Prev>);
  (RES (e.Ctx)) s.Id (e.Can) (e.In) t.Prev = (RES <DsSetActive (e.Ctx) t.Prev>);
  (FAILS (e.Ctx)) s.Id (e.Can) (e.In) t.Prev = (FAILS <DsSetActive (e.Ctx) t.Prev>);
  (ERR) s.Id (e.Can) (e.In) t.Prev = (ERR);
}

* Matching cannot decide this configuration, but the argument can be
* partitioned into cases that it can. A driver that stops at an unknown
* argument cannot compile anything whose input is not already ground.
DsUndecided {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags), <DsFlagsAny (e.Flags)> : '1' =
    <DsBlocked (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In)>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Flags) = (FAILS (e.Ctx));
}

DsBlocked {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) = <DsBlocked2 <DsSplit (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In)>>;
}

DsBlocked2 {
  (SOME (e.Call) (e.Ctx)) = (RED (e.Call) (e.Ctx));
  (NONE (e.Ctx)) = (RES (e.Ctx));
  (ERR) = (ERR);
}

* ---- the configuration record and the call log

DsRecConf {
  (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In) = <DsRecConf2 (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In) 0 <DsConfs (e.Ctx)>>;
}

DsRecConf2 {
  (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In) s.Next () = <DsRecConfNew (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In) s.Next>;
  (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In) s.Next ((CF s.Cid s.Id (e.In)) e.Rest) = (CID s.Cid (e.Ctx));
  (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In) s.Next (t.C e.Rest) =
    <DsRecConf2 (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In) <Add s.Next 1> (e.Rest)>;
}

DsRecConfNew {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE))
      s.Id (e.In) s.Next =
    (CID s.Next
      (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP)
          (e.CF (CF s.Next s.Id (e.In))) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE)));
}

DsRecCall {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) (NONE) s.N (e.WE))
      (e.Callee) (e.In) =
    (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) (NONE) s.N (e.WE));
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) (SOME s.From) s.N (e.WE))
      (e.Callee) (e.In),
    <DsHasTr (e.CT) s.From (e.Callee) (e.In)> : '1' =
      (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT)
          (SOME s.From) s.N (e.WE));
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) (SOME s.From) s.N (e.WE))
      (e.Callee) (e.In) =
    (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF)
        (e.CT (CFT s.From (e.Callee) (e.In) (NONE))) (SOME s.From) s.N (e.WE));
}

DsHasTr {
  (e.CT) s.From (e.Callee) (e.In) = <DsHasTrL (e.CT) s.From (e.Callee) (e.In)>;
}

DsHasTrL {
  () s.From (e.Callee) (e.In) = ;
  ((CFT s.From (e.C) (e.In)) e.Rest) s.From (e.Callee) (e.In),
    <SameChars (<Upper e.C>) (<Upper e.Callee>)> : '1' = '1';
  (t.T e.Rest) s.From (e.Callee) (e.In) = <DsHasTrL (e.Rest) s.From (e.Callee) (e.In)>;
}

* ---- the three-valued matcher

DsMatchSym {
  ((VAR 'e' e.N)) ((VAR s.K e.M)) (e.B) =
    (YES (e.B (VE (<Canon e.N>) ((VAR s.K e.M)))));
  (e.Pat) (e.In) (e.B), <DsAnySymVar (e.In)> : '1' =
    <DsMatchShape (e.Pat) (e.In) (e.B)>;
  (e.Pat) (e.In) (e.B) = <DsMatchGround (e.Pat) (e.In) (e.B)>;
}

DsMatchGround {
  (e.Pat) (e.In) (e.B), <DvMatch (e.Pat) (e.In) (e.B)> : (OK (e.B2)) = (YES (e.B2));
  (e.Pat) (e.In) (e.B) = (NO);
}

DsMatchShape {
  (e.Pat) (e.In) (e.B) = <DsMSAt (e.Pat) (e.In) (e.B)>;
}

DsMSAt {
  () (e.Ir) (e.B) = <DsMSEnd (e.Ir) (e.B)>;
  ((VAR 'e' e.N) e.Pr) (e.Ir) (e.B) = <DsMSExpr (e.N) (e.Pr) (e.Ir) (e.B)>;
  ((VAR s.K e.N) e.Pr) (e.Ir) (e.B) = <DsMSVar s.K (e.N) (e.Pr) (e.Ir) (e.B)>;
  (t.P e.Pr) (e.Ir) (e.B) = <DsMSLit t.P (e.Pr) (e.Ir) (e.B)>;
}

* An exhausted pattern with input left over is a definite no unless the tail is
* entirely `e.`-variables, because only they can denote nothing. `[]` against
* `[e.X]` is undecided; `[]` against `[(e.B)]` is a definite no.
DsMSEnd {
  () (e.B) = (YES (e.B));
  (e.Ir) (e.B), <DsAnyNonExpr (e.Ir)> : '1' = (NO);
  (e.Ir) (e.B) = (UNK);
}

DsAnyNonExpr {
  () = ;
  ((VAR 'e' e.N) e.Rest) = <DsAnyNonExpr (e.Rest)>;
  (t.X e.Rest) = '1';
}

* Expression splits are tried LONGEST prefix first, and that order decides which
* branch a sentence takes and therefore the whole residue. `DvSplits` builds
* shortest-first because the ground matcher wants that, so this reverses it.
DsMSExpr {
  (e.N) (e.Pr) (e.Ir) (e.B) =
    <DsMSExprL (e.N) (e.Pr) <DvRev (<DvSplits (e.Ir)>)> (e.B) '0'>;
}

DsMSExprL {
  (e.N) (e.Pr) () (e.B) s.U = <DsMSExprDone s.U>;
  (e.N) (e.Pr) (((e.Slice) (e.Suf)) e.More) (e.B) s.U,
    <DvLookup (e.B) (<Canon e.N>)> : (SOME (e.Old)), <SameChars (e.Old) (e.Slice)> : '1' =
      <DsMSExprStep <DsMSAt (e.Pr) (e.Suf) (e.B)>
                    (e.N) (e.Pr) (e.More) (e.B) s.U>;
  (e.N) (e.Pr) (((e.Slice) (e.Suf)) e.More) (e.B) s.U,
    <DvLookup (e.B) (<Canon e.N>)> : (SOME (e.Old)) =
      <DsMSExprL (e.N) (e.Pr) (e.More) (e.B) s.U>;
  (e.N) (e.Pr) (((e.Slice) (e.Suf)) e.More) (e.B) s.U =
      <DsMSExprStep <DsMSAt (e.Pr) (e.Suf) (e.B (VE (<Canon e.N>) (e.Slice)))>
                    (e.N) (e.Pr) (e.More) (e.B) s.U>;
}

DsMSExprStep {
  (YES (e.B2)) (e.N) (e.Pr) (e.More) (e.B) s.U = (YES (e.B2));
  (UNK) (e.N) (e.Pr) (e.More) (e.B) s.U =
    <DsMSExprL (e.N) (e.Pr) (e.More) (e.B) '1'>;
  (NO) (e.N) (e.Pr) (e.More) (e.B) s.U =
    <DsMSExprL (e.N) (e.Pr) (e.More) (e.B) s.U>;
}

DsMSExprDone {
  '1' = (UNK);
  s.U = (NO);
}

DsMSVar {
  s.K (e.N) (e.Pr) () (e.B) = (NO);
  s.K (e.N) (e.Pr) (t.I e.Ir) (e.B), <DsVarAccepts s.K t.I> : (SOME '1') =
    <DsMSVarBind s.K (e.N) (e.Pr) (e.Ir) t.I (e.B)>;
  s.K (e.N) (e.Pr) (t.I e.Ir) (e.B), <DsVarAccepts s.K t.I> : (SOME '0') = (NO);
  s.K (e.N) (e.Pr) (t.I e.Ir) (e.B) = (UNK);
}

DsMSVarBind {
  s.K (e.N) (e.Pr) (e.Ir) t.I (e.B),
    <DvLookup (e.B) (<Canon e.N>)> : (SOME (e.Old)), <SameChars (e.Old) (t.I)> : '1' =
      <DsMSAt (e.Pr) (e.Ir) (e.B)>;
  s.K (e.N) (e.Pr) (e.Ir) t.I (e.B),
    <DvLookup (e.B) (<Canon e.N>)> : (SOME (e.Old)) = (NO);
  s.K (e.N) (e.Pr) (e.Ir) t.I (e.B) =
      <DsMSAt (e.Pr) (e.Ir) (e.B (VE (<Canon e.N>) (t.I)))>;
}

DsMSLit {
  t.P (e.Pr) () (e.B) = (NO);
  t.P (e.Pr) (t.I e.Ir) (e.B), <DsMSTerm t.P t.I (e.B)> : (YES (e.B2)) =
    <DsMSAt (e.Pr) (e.Ir) (e.B2)>;
  t.P (e.Pr) (t.I e.Ir) (e.B), <DsMSTerm t.P t.I (e.B)> : (UNK) = (UNK);
  t.P (e.Pr) (t.I e.Ir) (e.B) = (NO);
}

DsMSTerm {
  (BR e.P) (BR e.I) (e.B) = <DsMatchShape (e.P) (e.I) (e.B)>;
  t.P t.I (e.B), <DsShape t.P> : (BR), <DsShape t.I> : (SYM) = (NO);
  t.P t.I (e.B), <DsShape t.P> : (SYM), <DsShape t.I> : (BR) = (NO);
  t.P t.I (e.B), <DsContainsSym t.I> : '1' = (UNK);
  t.P t.I (e.B), <DvGround t.P t.I> : '1' = (YES (e.B));
  t.P t.I (e.B) = (NO);
}

DsShape {
  (BR e.X) = (BR);
  (SYM s.C) = (SYM);
  (NUM e.D) = (SYM);
  (ID e.N) = (SYM);
  (VAR 's' e.N) = (SYM);
  t.X = (UNK);
}

* The kind lattice. `s.` ranges over symbols -- character, number or identifier
* -- but never a bracket; `t.` over any single term; `e.` over any expression.
* Anything wider is None, i.e. Unknown, and guessing there is what makes a
* driver unsound.
DsVarAccepts {
  's' (SYM s.C) = (SOME '1');
  's' (ID e.N) = (SOME '1');
  's' (NUM e.D) = (SOME '1');
  's' (BR e.X) = (SOME '0');
  's' (VAR 's' e.N) = (SOME '1');
  's' t.X = (NONE);
  't' (SYM s.C) = (SOME '1');
  't' (ID e.N) = (SOME '1');
  't' (NUM e.D) = (SOME '1');
  't' (BR e.X) = (SOME '1');
  't' (VAR 'e' e.N) = (NONE);
  't' (VAR s.K e.N) = (SOME '1');
  't' t.X = (NONE);
}

* Whether the input contains any symbolic variable, which is what decides
* between the shape matcher and the ground one.
DsAnySymVar {
  () = ;
  (t.X e.Rest), <DsContainsSym t.X> : '1' = '1';
  (t.X e.Rest) = <DsAnySymVar (e.Rest)>;
}

DsContainsSym {
  (VAR s.K e.N) = '1';
  (BR e.X) = <DsAnySymVar (e.X)>;
  (CALL (ID e.N) e.Args) = <DsAnySymVar (e.Args)>;
  (BLK (e.Arg) e.Sents) = <DsSymInBlk (e.Arg) (e.Sents)>;
  t.X = ;
}

DsSymInBlk {
  (e.Arg) (e.Sents), <DsAnySymVar (e.Arg)> : '1' = '1';
  (e.Arg) () = ;
  (e.Arg) ((SENT (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Res)) e.More),
    <DsAnySymVar (e.Pat)> : '1' = '1';
  (e.Arg) ((SENT (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Res)) e.More),
    <DsAnySymVar (e.Res)> : '1' = '1';
  (e.Arg) ((SENT (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Res)) e.More),
    <DsSymVarConds (e.Conds)> : '1' = '1';
  (e.Arg) (t.S e.More) = <DsSymInBlk (e.Arg) (e.More)>;
}

DsSymVarConds {
  () = ;
  ((COND (e.Expr) (e.CPat)) e.More), <DsAnySymVar (e.Expr)> : '1' = '1';
  ((COND (e.Expr) (e.CPat)) e.More), <DsAnySymVar (e.CPat)> : '1' = '1';
  (t.C e.More) = <DsSymVarConds (e.More)>;
}

* ---- conditions

DsMatchConds {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) () = (MY (e.B) (e.Ctx));
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) ((COND (e.Expr) ((BLK (e.BArg) e.Sents))) e.More) =
    <DsCondsBlk (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Expr) (e.Sents) (e.More)>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) ((COND (e.Expr) (e.CPat)) e.More) =
    <DsCondsVal (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Expr) (e.CPat) (e.More)>;
}

DsCondsVal {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Expr) (e.CPat) (e.More) =
    <DsCondsVal2 <DsInstantiate (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Expr)> (e.B) (e.CPat) (e.More)>;
}

DsCondsVal2 {
  (RED (e.Val) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.CPat) (e.More) =
    <DsCondsVal3 <DsMatchSym (e.CPat) (e.Val) (e.B)> (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.More)>;
  (RES (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.CPat) (e.More) = (MU (e.Ctx));
  (FAILS (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.CPat) (e.More) = (MN (e.Ctx));
  (ERR) (e.B) (e.CPat) (e.More) = (ERR);
}

DsCondsVal3 {
  (YES (e.B2)) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.More) = <DsMatchConds (e.Ctx) (e.B2) (e.More)>;
  (NO) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.More) = (MN (e.Ctx));
  (UNK) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.More) = (MU (e.Ctx));
}

* A block in condition position is an anonymous function applied to the value,
* so it is called rather than matched. The bindings it makes stay inside it,
* which is why the block is tested against the enclosing bindings but never
* writes back to them.
DsCondsBlk {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Expr) (e.Sents) (e.More) =
    <DsCondsBlkA <DsInstantiate (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Expr)> (e.B) (e.Sents) (e.More)>;
}

DsCondsBlkA {
  (RED (e.Val) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Sents) (e.More) =
    <DsBlkCond (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Sents) (e.More)>;
  (RES (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Sents) (e.More) = (MU (e.Ctx));
  (FAILS (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Sents) (e.More) = (MN (e.Ctx));
  (ERR) (e.B) (e.Sents) (e.More) = (ERR);
}

DsBlkCond {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Sents) (e.More) =
    <DsBlkCondL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Sents) (e.More) '0'>;
}

DsBlkCondL {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) () (e.More) s.U = <DsBlkCondDone s.U (e.Ctx)>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) ((SENT (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Res)) e.Rest) (e.More) s.U =
    <DsBlkCond2 <DsMatchSym (e.Pat) (e.Val) (e.B)>
                (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Conds) (e.Rest) (e.More) s.U>;
}

DsBlkCond2 {
  (NO) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Conds) (e.Rest) (e.More) s.U =
    <DsBlkCondL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Rest) (e.More) s.U>;
  (UNK) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Conds) (e.Rest) (e.More) s.U =
    <DsBlkCondL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Rest) (e.More) '1'>;
  (YES (e.B2)) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Conds) (e.Rest) (e.More) s.U =
    <DsBlkCond3 <DsMatchConds (e.Ctx) (e.B2) (e.Conds)> (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Rest) (e.More) s.U>;
}

DsBlkCond3 {
  (MY (e.B3) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Rest) (e.More) s.U = (MY (e.B) (e.Ctx));
  (MN (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Rest) (e.More) s.U =
    <DsBlkCondL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Rest) (e.More) s.U>;
  (MU (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Rest) (e.More) s.U =
    <DsBlkCondL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Rest) (e.More) '1'>;
  (ERR) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Rest) (e.More) s.U = (ERR);
}

DsBlkCondDone {
  '1' (e.Ctx) = (MU (e.Ctx));
  s.U (e.Ctx) = (MN (e.Ctx));
}

* ---- instantiation

DsInstantiate {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Terms) = <DsInstL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Terms) ()>;
}

DsInstL {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) () (e.Acc) = (RED (e.Acc) (e.Ctx));
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (t.T e.Rest) (e.Acc) = <DsInstOne (e.Ctx) (e.B) t.T (e.Rest) (e.Acc)>;
}

DsInstOne {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (VAR s.K e.N) (e.Rest) (e.Acc),
    <DvLookup (e.B) (<Canon e.N>)> : (SOME (e.V)) =
      <DsInstL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc e.V)>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (VAR s.K e.N) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) = (ERR);
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (BR e.Inner) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) =
    <DsInstBr (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Inner) (e.Rest) (e.Acc)>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (CALL (ID e.N) e.Args) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) =
    <DsInstCall (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.N) (e.Args) (e.Rest) (e.Acc)>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (BLK (e.Arg) e.Sents) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) =
    <DsInstBlk (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Arg) (e.Sents) (e.Rest) (e.Acc)>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) t.T (e.Rest) (e.Acc) = <DsInstL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc t.T)>;
}

DsInstBr {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Inner) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) =
    <DsInstBr2 <DsInstantiate (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Inner)> (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc)>;
}

DsInstBr2 {
  (RED (e.In) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) =
    <DsInstL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc (BR e.In))>;
  (RES (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) = (RES (e.Ctx));
  (FAILS (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) = (RES (e.Ctx));
  (ERR) (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) = (ERR);
}

DsInstCall {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.N) (e.Args) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) =
    <DsInstCall2 <DsInstantiate (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Args)> (e.B) (e.N) (e.Rest) (e.Acc)>;
}

DsInstCall2 {
  (RED (e.ArgsOut) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.N) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) =
    <DsInstCall3 <DsInvoke (e.Ctx) (e.N) (e.ArgsOut)> (e.B) (e.N) (e.ArgsOut) (e.Rest) (e.Acc)>;
  (RES (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.N) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) = (RES (e.Ctx));
  (FAILS (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.N) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) = (RES (e.Ctx));
  (ERR) (e.B) (e.N) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) = (ERR);
}

* A call that cannot succeed is still part of the residue: dropping it would
* turn a program that fails at run time into one that quietly returns something
* else.
DsInstCall3 {
  (RED (e.Out) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.N) (e.ArgsOut) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) =
    <DsInstL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc e.Out)>;
  (RES (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.N) (e.ArgsOut) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) =
    <DsInstL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc (CALL (ID e.N) e.ArgsOut))>;
  (FAILS (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.N) (e.ArgsOut) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) =
    <DsInstL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc (CALL (ID e.N) e.ArgsOut))>;
  (ERR) (e.B) (e.N) (e.ArgsOut) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) = (ERR);
}

DsInstBlk {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Arg) (e.Sents) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) =
    <DsInstBlk2 <DsInstantiate (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Arg)> (e.B) (e.Sents) (e.Rest) (e.Acc)>;
}

DsInstBlk2 {
  (RED (e.Val) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Sents) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) =
    <DsInstBlk3 <DsBlkSym (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Sents)> (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc)>;
  (RES (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Sents) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) = (RES (e.Ctx));
  (FAILS (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Sents) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) = (RES (e.Ctx));
  (ERR) (e.B) (e.Sents) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) = (ERR);
}

DsInstBlk3 {
  (RED (e.Out) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) =
    <DsInstL (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc e.Out)>;
  (RES (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) = (RES (e.Ctx));
  (FAILS (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) = (RES (e.Ctx));
  (ERR) (e.B) (e.Rest) (e.Acc) = (ERR);
}

DsBlkSym {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Arg) (e.Sents) =
    <DsBlkSymA <DsInstantiate (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Arg)> (e.B) (e.Sents)>;
}

DsBlkSymA {
  (RED (e.Val) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Sents) = <DsBlkSymB (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Sents) '0'>;
  (RES (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Sents) = (RES (e.Ctx));
  (FAILS (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Sents) = (RES (e.Ctx));
  (ERR) (e.B) (e.Sents) = (ERR);
}

DsBlkSymB {
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) () s.U = (RES (e.Ctx));
  (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) ((SENT (e.Pat) (e.Conds) (e.Res)) e.More) s.U =
    <DsBlkSymC <DsMatchSym (e.Pat) (e.Val) (e.B)>
               (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.More) s.U>;
}

DsBlkSymC {
  (NO) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.More) s.U =
    <DsBlkSymB (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.More) s.U>;
  (UNK) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.More) s.U =
    <DsBlkSymB (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.More) '1'>;
  (YES (e.B2)) (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Conds) (e.Res) (e.More) s.U =
    <DsBlkSymD <DsMatchConds (e.Ctx) (e.B2) (e.Conds)> (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Res) (e.More) s.U>;
}

DsBlkSymD {
  (MN (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Res) (e.More) s.U =
    <DsBlkSymB (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.More) s.U>;
  (MU (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Res) (e.More) s.U =
    <DsBlkSymB (e.Ctx) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.More) '1'>;
  (MY (e.B2) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Res) (e.More) '1' = (RES (e.Ctx));
  (MY (e.B2) (e.Ctx)) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Res) (e.More) s.U =
    <DsInstantiate (e.Ctx) (e.B2) (e.Res)>;
  (ERR) (e.B) (e.Val) (e.Res) (e.More) s.U = (ERR);
}

* ---- case splitting

* A configuration whose input still contains an unevaluated call or a block is
* not a value the callee has been handed: a split's sentences use the input as
* their PATTERN, and a call is not a term a Refal pattern may contain, so
* splitting there emits a residue the compiler rejects -- `<F (<Chr 10>) e.X>`
* partitions to a sentence whose pattern is `(<Chr 10>)`. The call is left
* residual instead, which is what the source does with it. This is the same
* characterisability test the entering restrictions use, for the same reason.
DsSplit {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In), <DsCharisable (e.In)> : '1' =
    <DsSplitPos (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) <DsExprVarPos (e.In) 0 ()>>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) = (NONE (e.Ctx));
}

DsCharisable {
  (e.Terms) = <DsCharisL e.Terms>;
}

DsCharisL {
  = '1';
  (CALL e.Args) e.Rest = ;
  (BLK e.Args) e.Rest = ;
  (BR e.Inner) e.Rest = <DsCharisBR (<DsCharisL e.Inner>) e.Rest>;
  t.X e.Rest = <DsCharisL e.Rest>;
}

* A bracket is characterisable only when its contents are, and the two cases
* must not be merged into one sentence with a condition: a bracket whose
* contents are *not* characterisable would then fall through to the catch-all
* below, which looks only at the rest of the list and answers yes.
DsCharisBR {
  ('1') e.Rest = <DsCharisL e.Rest>;
  (e.Other) e.Rest = ;
}

DsExprVarPos {
  () s.I (e.Acc) = (e.Acc);
  ((VAR 'e' e.N) e.Rest) s.I (e.Acc) = <DsExprVarPos (e.Rest) <Add s.I 1> (e.Acc s.I)>;
  (t.T e.Rest) s.I (e.Acc) = <DsExprVarPos (e.Rest) <Add s.I 1> (e.Acc)>;
}

* Only a single top-level expression variable is partitioned. Splitting one of
* several would leave the others undecided and grow the residue without making
* progress.
DsSplitPos {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (s.P) = <DsSplitGo (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.P>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) (e.Other) = (NONE (e.Ctx));
}

DsSplitGo {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.P, <DsHasFunc (e.Ctx) (e.Fn)> : '1' =
    <DsSplitEntry (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.P>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.P = (NONE (e.Ctx));
}

* The entry is only split when it accepts an arbitrary expression. For a
* narrower entry the residue would have to reuse that pattern, which a partition
* of the variable does not fit -- and splitting anyway would produce a residue
* that accepts more than the source did, turning a failing program into a
* looping one. So a matching entry state whose pattern is NOT a single
* expression variable is the refusal, and it is the only refusal.
DsSplitEntry {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.P, <DsEntryIsVar (e.Ctx) (e.Fn)> : '1' =
    <DsSplitFind (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.P>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.P = (NONE (e.Ctx));
}

DsEntryIsVar {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) = <DsEntryIsVar2 <DsStates (e.Ctx)> <DsEntry (e.Ctx)> (e.Fn)>;
}

DsEntryIsVar2 {
  () s.E (e.Fn) = '1';
  ((ST s.E (e.N) s.J (SENT ((VAR 'e' e.M)) (e.C) (e.R))) e.Rest) s.E (e.Fn),
    <SameChars (<Upper e.N>) (<Upper e.Fn>)> : '1' = '1';
  ((ST s.E (e.N) s.J t.Sent) e.Rest) s.E (e.Fn),
    <SameChars (<Upper e.N>) (<Upper e.Fn>)> : '1' = ;
  (t.S e.Rest) s.E (e.Fn) = <DsEntryIsVar2 (e.Rest) s.E (e.Fn)>;
}

DsSplitFind {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.P = <DsSplitFind2 (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.P <DsCanonical (e.In)>>;
}

DsSplitFind2 {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.P (e.Can),
    <DsFindSplit (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.Can)> : (SOME (e.Name)) =
      (SOME <DsCallSeq (e.Name) (e.In)> (e.Ctx));
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.P (e.Can),
    <DsSplitCount (e.Ctx)> : s.N, <Compare s.N 16> : '-' =
      <DsSplitMake (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.P (e.Can) <Add s.N 1>>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.P (e.Can) = (NONE (e.Ctx));
}

DsSplitCount {
  (e.Ctx) = <DsLen <DsSplits (e.Ctx)>>;
}

DsFindSplit {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.Can) = <DsFindSplitL <DsSplits (e.Ctx)> (e.Fn) (e.Can)>;
}

DsFindSplitL {
  () (e.Fn) (e.Can) = (NONE);
  ((SP (e.Name) (e.F) (e.Can) (e.Sents)) e.Rest) (e.Fn) (e.Can),
    <SameChars (<Upper e.F>) (<Upper e.Fn>)> : '1' = (SOME (e.Name));
  (t.S e.Rest) (e.Fn) (e.Can) = <DsFindSplitL (e.Rest) (e.Fn) (e.Can)>;
}

DsSplitMake {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.P (e.Can) s.Index =
    <DsSplitMake2 <DsAddSplit (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.Can) (e.In) s.Index>
                  (e.Fn) (e.In) s.P s.Index>;
}

DsSplitMake2 {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.P s.Index =
    <DsSplitDrive (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Index
      <DsReplace (e.In) s.P ()>
      <DsReplace (e.In) s.P ((VAR 's' <DsHName s.Index>) (VAR 'e' <DsTName s.Index>))>
      <DsReplace (e.In) s.P ((BR (VAR 'e' <DsBName s.Index>)) (VAR 'e' <DsTName s.Index>))>>;
}

DsSplitDrive {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Index (e.B1) (e.B2) (e.B3) =
    <DsSplitLoop (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Index ((e.B1) (e.B2) (e.B3)) ()>;
}

DsSplitLoop {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Index () (e.Acc) =
    <DsSplitFinish (e.Ctx) s.Index (e.Acc) (e.In)>;
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Index ((e.B) e.More) (e.Acc) =
    <DsSplitOne <DsInvoke (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.B)> (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Index (e.B) (e.More) (e.Acc)>;
}

* A branch that no sentence matches keeps a call to the original function, so
* the residue fails exactly where the source fails instead of inventing an
* answer.
* The branch is a list of terms, not one bracketed term, so the sentence's
* pattern bracket holds `e.B` itself. Writing `((e.B))` instead would put a
* bracket inside a bracket: the empty branch would come out as a pattern of one
* empty bracket rather than an empty pattern, and the residue would no longer
* accept what the source accepted. Nothing rendered a split sentence until
* `residualize-driven` did, which is why the extra pair survived this long.
DsSplitOne {
  (RED (e.Out) (e.Ctx)) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Index (e.B) (e.More) (e.Acc) =
    <DsSplitLoop (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Index (e.More) (e.Acc (SENT (e.B) () (e.Out)))>;
  (RES (e.Ctx)) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Index (e.B) (e.More) (e.Acc) =
    <DsSplitLoop (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Index (e.More)
                 (e.Acc (SENT (e.B) () <DsCallSeq (e.Fn) (e.B)>))>;
  (FAILS (e.Ctx)) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Index (e.B) (e.More) (e.Acc) =
    <DsSplitLoop (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Index (e.More)
                 (e.Acc (SENT (e.B) () <DsCallSeq (e.Fn) (e.B)>))>;
  (ERR) (e.Fn) (e.In) s.Index (e.B) (e.More) (e.Acc) = (ERR);
}

DsSplitFinish {
  (e.Ctx) s.Index (e.Sents) (e.In) =
    (SOME <DsCallSeq (<DsSplitName s.Index>) (e.In)>
          <DsSetSents <DsPop (e.Ctx)> s.Index (e.Sents)>);
}

DsAddSplit {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE))
      (e.Fn) (e.Can) (e.In) s.Index =
    (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI)
        (e.AP (AP (e.Fn) (e.Can) (e.In) <DsNeigh (e.In)> (SOME (<DsSplitName s.Index>))))
        (e.SP (SP (<DsSplitName s.Index>) (e.Fn) (e.Can) ()))
        (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE));
}

DsSetSents {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE))
      s.Index (e.Sents) =
    (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP)
        <DsSetSentsL (e.SP) s.Index (e.Sents) 1 ()>
        (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE));
}

DsSetSentsL {
  () s.Index (e.Sents) s.I (e.Acc) = (e.Acc);
  ((SP (e.Name) (e.Fn) (e.Can) (e.Old)) e.Rest) s.Index (e.Sents) s.I (e.Acc),
    <Compare s.I s.Index> : '0' = (e.Acc (SP (e.Name) (e.Fn) (e.Can) (e.Sents)) e.Rest);
  ((SP (e.Name) (e.Fn) (e.Can) (e.Old)) e.Rest) s.Index (e.Sents) s.I (e.Acc) =
    <DsSetSentsL (e.Rest) s.Index (e.Sents) <Add s.I 1>
                       (e.Acc (SP (e.Name) (e.Fn) (e.Can) (e.Old)))>;
}

* A name in the AST is a CHARACTER SEQUENCE, not one identifier symbol: `Parse`
* builds `(VAR s.K e.I)` and `(ID e.N)` from the lexer's word characters, and the
* ground driver's binding keys are `<Canon e.I>` over those characters. Building
* a name with `Implode` would make it a single identifier symbol instead, which
* `Canon` would hand to `Ord` whole and `Compare` would reject -- and it would
* stop `SameChars` from matching it against a name that came from the source.
DsSplitName {
  s.I = 'S' 'p' 'l' 'i' 't' <Symb s.I>;
}

DsHName {
  s.I = 'H' <Symb s.I>;
}

DsTName {
  s.I = 'T' <Symb s.I>;
}

DsBName {
  s.I = 'B' <Symb s.I>;
}

DsReplace {
  (e.In) s.P (e.Repl) = <DsReplaceL (e.In) s.P (e.Repl) ()>;
}

DsReplaceL {
  () s.P (e.Repl) (e.Acc) = (e.Acc);
  (t.X e.Rest) 0 (e.Repl) (e.Acc) = (e.Acc e.Repl e.Rest);
  (t.X e.Rest) s.P (e.Repl) (e.Acc) =
    <DsReplaceL (e.Rest) <Sub s.P 1> (e.Repl) (e.Acc t.X)>;
}

DsHasFunc {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Callee) = <DsHasFuncL <DsStates (e.Ctx)> (e.Callee)>;
}

DsHasFuncL {
  () (e.Callee) = ;
  ((ST s.Id (e.N) s.J t.Sent) e.Rest) (e.Callee),
    <SameChars (<Upper e.N>) (<Upper e.Callee>)> : '1' = '1';
  (t.S e.Rest) (e.Callee) = <DsHasFuncL (e.Rest) (e.Callee)>;
}

* ---- canonical form, residue reuse, and the embedding test

DsCanonical {
  (e.In) = <DsCanonOut <DsCanonL (e.In) () ()>>;
}

DsCanonOut {
  (CAN (e.Acc) (e.Seen)) = (e.Acc);
}

* Variable names are erased in order of first appearance, so a configuration
* that renamed its variables on the way round is recognised as the same piece of
* work. The seen list is threaded back out of nested brackets, because a
* variable first met inside one continues the numbering outside it.
DsCanonL {
  () (e.Seen) (e.Acc) = (CAN (e.Acc) (e.Seen));
  ((VAR s.K e.N) e.Rest) (e.Seen) (e.Acc),
    <DsFindName (e.Seen) (<Canon e.N>)> : (SOME s.I) =
      <DsCanonL (e.Rest) (e.Seen) (e.Acc (VAR s.K <DsVName s.I>))>;
  ((VAR s.K e.N) e.Rest) (e.Seen) (e.Acc) =
      <DsCanonL (e.Rest) (e.Seen (<Canon e.N>))
                (e.Acc (VAR s.K <DsVName <DsLen (e.Seen)>>))>;
  ((BR e.X) e.Rest) (e.Seen) (e.Acc) =
      <DsCanonBr (e.Rest) (e.Acc) <DsCanonL (e.X) (e.Seen) ()>>;
  ((CALL (ID e.N) e.Args) e.Rest) (e.Seen) (e.Acc) =
      <DsCanonCall (e.Rest) (e.Acc) (e.N) <DsCanonL (e.Args) (e.Seen) ()>>;
  (t.T e.Rest) (e.Seen) (e.Acc) = <DsCanonL (e.Rest) (e.Seen) (e.Acc t.T)>;
}

DsCanonBr {
  (e.Rest) (e.Acc) (CAN (e.In) (e.Seen2)) =
    <DsCanonL (e.Rest) (e.Seen2) (e.Acc (BR e.In))>;
}

DsCanonCall {
  (e.Rest) (e.Acc) (e.N) (CAN (e.In) (e.Seen2)) =
    <DsCanonL (e.Rest) (e.Seen2) (e.Acc (CALL (ID e.N) e.In))>;
}

DsFindName {
  (e.Seen) (e.Name) = <DsFindNameL (e.Seen) (e.Name) 0>;
}

DsFindNameL {
  () (e.Name) s.I = (NONE);
  ((e.Name) e.Rest) (e.Name) s.I = (SOME s.I);
  (t.X e.Rest) (e.Name) s.I = <DsFindNameL (e.Rest) (e.Name) <Add s.I 1>>;
}

DsLen {
  (e.L) = <DsLenL (e.L) 0>;
}

DsLenL {
  () s.N = s.N;
  (t.X e.Rest) s.N = <DsLenL (e.Rest) <Add s.N 1>>;
}

DsVName {
  s.I = 'V' <Symb s.I>;
}

DsSameConf {
  (e.A) (e.B) = <SameChars <DsCanonical (e.A)> <DsCanonical (e.B)>>;
}

* Reuse the residual already computed for an alpha-equivalent configuration.
* Because both arguments are canonical they are structurally identical, so their
* variables pair up positionally and the stored residue can be renamed to speak
* the current argument's names -- which is what makes the reuse exact.
DsCompleted {
  (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In) =
    <DsCompletedL <DsCompletedList (e.Ctx)> s.Id (e.In) <DsCanonical (e.In)>>;
}

DsCompletedL {
  (e.CP) s.Id (e.In) (e.Can) = <DsCompletedL2 (e.CP) s.Id (e.In) (e.Can)>;
}

DsCompletedL2 {
  () s.Id (e.In) (e.Can) = (NONE);
  ((CP s.Id (e.Can) (e.Stored) (e.Res)) e.Rest) s.Id (e.In) (e.Can),
    <SameChars (e.Stored) (e.In)> : '1' = (SOME (e.Res));
  ((CP s.Id (e.Can) (e.Stored) (e.Res)) e.Rest) s.Id (e.In) (e.Can) =
    (SOME <DsRename (e.Res) <DsVarPairs (e.Stored) (e.In) ()>>);
  (t.C e.Rest) s.Id (e.In) (e.Can) = <DsCompletedL2 (e.Rest) s.Id (e.In) (e.Can)>;
}

DsVarPairs {
  (e.Stored) (e.Current) (e.Acc) = <DsVarPairsL (e.Stored) (e.Current) (e.Acc)>;
}

DsVarPairsL {
  () (e.C) (e.Acc) = (e.Acc);
  ((VAR s.K e.L) e.SRest) ((VAR s.K2 e.R) e.CRest) (e.Acc) =
    <DsVarPairsL (e.SRest) (e.CRest) (e.Acc (VP (<Canon e.L>) (<Canon e.R>)))>;
  ((BR e.L) e.SRest) ((BR e.R) e.CRest) (e.Acc) =
    <DsVarPairsL (e.SRest) (e.CRest) <DsVarPairs (e.L) (e.R) (e.Acc)>>;
  ((CALL (ID e.N) e.L) e.SRest) ((CALL (ID e.N2) e.R) e.CRest) (e.Acc) =
    <DsVarPairsL (e.SRest) (e.CRest) <DsVarPairs (e.L) (e.R) (e.Acc)>>;
  (t.L e.SRest) (t.R e.CRest) (e.Acc) = <DsVarPairsL (e.SRest) (e.CRest) (e.Acc)>;
}

DsRename {
  (e.Terms) (e.Pairs) = <DsRenameL (e.Terms) (e.Pairs) ()>;
}

DsRenameL {
  () (e.Pairs) (e.Acc) = (e.Acc);
  ((VAR s.K e.N) e.Rest) (e.Pairs) (e.Acc),
    <DsPairTo (e.Pairs) (<Canon e.N>)> : (SOME (e.To)) =
      <DsRenameL (e.Rest) (e.Pairs) (e.Acc (VAR s.K e.To))>;
  ((VAR s.K e.N) e.Rest) (e.Pairs) (e.Acc) =
      <DsRenameL (e.Rest) (e.Pairs) (e.Acc (VAR s.K e.N))>;
  ((BR e.X) e.Rest) (e.Pairs) (e.Acc) =
      <DsRenameL (e.Rest) (e.Pairs) (e.Acc (BR <DsRename (e.X) (e.Pairs)>))>;
  ((CALL (ID e.N) e.Args) e.Rest) (e.Pairs) (e.Acc) =
      <DsRenameL (e.Rest) (e.Pairs) (e.Acc (CALL (ID e.N) <DsRename (e.Args) (e.Pairs)>))>;
  (t.T e.Rest) (e.Pairs) (e.Acc) = <DsRenameL (e.Rest) (e.Pairs) (e.Acc t.T)>;
}

DsPairTo {
  (e.Pairs) (e.Key) = <DsPairToL (e.Pairs) (e.Key)>;
}

DsPairToL {
  () (e.Key) = (NONE);
  ((VP (e.Key) (e.To)) e.Rest) (e.Key) = (SOME (e.To));
  (t.P e.Rest) (e.Key) = <DsPairToL (e.Rest) (e.Key)>;
}

DsSeenSame {
  (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In) = <DsSeenSameL <DsVisitedIn (e.Ctx)> s.Id (e.In)>;
}

DsSeenSameL {
  () s.Id (e.In) = ;
  ((VI s.Id (e.Prev)) e.Rest) s.Id (e.In), <DsSameConf (e.Prev) (e.In)> : '1' = '1';
  (t.V e.Rest) s.Id (e.In) = <DsSeenSameL (e.Rest) s.Id (e.In)>;
}

* The first configuration at this state that is *not* the same one and into
* which the current argument embeds homeomorphically.
DsFindEmbedding {
  (e.Ctx) s.Id (e.In) = <DsEmbedFindL <DsVisitedIn (e.Ctx)> s.Id (e.In)>;
}

DsEmbedFindL {
  () s.Id (e.In) = (NONE);
  ((VI s.Id (e.Prev)) e.Rest) s.Id (e.In), <DsSameConf (e.Prev) (e.In)> : '1' =
    <DsEmbedFindL (e.Rest) s.Id (e.In)>;
  ((VI s.Id (e.Prev)) e.Rest) s.Id (e.In), <DsEmbeds (e.Prev) (e.In)> : '1' =
    (SOME (e.Prev));
  (t.V e.Rest) s.Id (e.In) = <DsEmbedFindL (e.Rest) s.Id (e.In)>;
}

DsActiveHas {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.Can) = <DsActiveHasL <DsActivePath (e.Ctx)> (e.Fn) (e.Can)>;
}

DsActiveHasL {
  () (e.Fn) (e.Can) = ;
  ((AP (e.F) (e.Can) (e.I) (e.N) t.S) e.Rest) (e.Fn) (e.Can),
    <SameChars (<Upper e.F>) (<Upper e.Fn>)> : '1' = '1';
  (t.A e.Rest) (e.Fn) (e.Can) = <DsActiveHasL (e.Rest) (e.Fn) (e.Can)>;
}

DsActiveSplit {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.Can) = <DsActiveSplitL <DsActivePath (e.Ctx)> (e.Fn) (e.Can)>;
}

DsActiveSplitL {
  () (e.Fn) (e.Can) = (NONE);
  ((AP (e.F) (e.Can) (e.I) (e.N) (SOME (e.Name))) e.Rest) (e.Fn) (e.Can),
    <SameChars (<Upper e.F>) (<Upper e.Fn>)> : '1' = (SOME (e.Name));
  (t.A e.Rest) (e.Fn) (e.Can) = <DsActiveSplitL (e.Rest) (e.Fn) (e.Can)>;
}

* Turchin's own loop-back rule (1988 section 4): loop back when a first-order
* neighborhood recurs, not merely when a configuration does.
DsActiveNeigh {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Fn) (e.In) =
    <DsActiveNeighL <DsActivePath (e.Ctx)> (e.Fn) <DsNeigh (e.In)> (e.In)>;
}

DsActiveNeighL {
  () (e.Fn) (e.N) (e.In) = (NONE);
  ((AP (e.F) (e.C) (e.I) (e.N) t.S) e.Rest) (e.Fn) (e.N) (e.In),
    <SameChars (<Upper e.F>) (<Upper e.Fn>)> : '1',
    <DsSameConf (e.I) (e.In)> : = (SOME (e.I));
  (t.A e.Rest) (e.Fn) (e.N) (e.In) = <DsActiveNeighL (e.Rest) (e.Fn) (e.N) (e.In)>;
}

* `record_whistle` accumulates whistle states, whistle inputs and whistle
* events. The symbolic driver's report prints none of them, which is why this
* was the identity while that was the only consumer. `residualize-driven` does
* print them -- `whistles`, `generalized` and `generalized-states` -- so the
* evidence is recorded here now.
*
* One list is enough. A state enters the Rust context's `whistle_states`
* exactly when it enters `whistle_events`, and in the same relative order: the
* first call for a state always carries a triple no earlier event had, and a
* repeat of a triple adds to neither. So `whistles` is the distinct states of
* the event list and `generalized` is its length, and the event list is the
* only thing that has to be threaded through the context.
DsWhistle {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE))
      s.Id (e.Prev) (e.In) =
    (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N
        <DsWhistleAdd (e.WE) s.Id (e.Prev) (e.In)>);
}

DsWhistleAdd {
  (e.WE) s.Id (e.Prev) (e.In) =
    <DsWhistleAdd2 (e.WE) s.Id (e.Prev) (e.In)
      <DsWhistleHas (e.WE) s.Id (e.Prev) (e.In)>>;
}

DsWhistleAdd2 {
  (e.WE) s.Id (e.Prev) (e.In) '1' = (e.WE);
  (e.WE) s.Id (e.Prev) (e.In) = (e.WE (WE s.Id (e.Prev) (e.In)));
}

* The three bound variables are repeated between the stored event and the
* arguments, so Refal's repeated-variable rule makes this an exact structural
* comparison -- which is what the Rust context does with the triple.
DsWhistleHas {
  () s.Id (e.Prev) (e.In) = ;
  ((WE s.Id (e.Prev) (e.In)) e.Rest) s.Id (e.Prev) (e.In) = '1';
  (t.E e.Rest) s.Id (e.Prev) (e.In) = <DsWhistleHas (e.Rest) s.Id (e.Prev) (e.In)>;
}

* ---- neighborhoods (Turchin 1988 section 3)

DsNeigh {
  (e.In) = <DsCanonical (<DsNeighOf (e.In)>)>;
}

DsNeighOf {
  () = (VAR 'e' 'N');
  (t.X e.Rest) = <DsNeighOf2 <DsContract t.X>>;
}

DsNeighOf2 {
  (NONE) = (VAR 'e' 'N');
  (SOME t.S) = t.S (VAR 'e' 'N');
}

* The contraction carries no information for an `e.`-variable, a call or a
* block -- they already stand for an unbounded part of the expression -- so the
* neighborhood then says only that everything from here on is unknown.
DsContract {
  (SYM s.C) = (SOME (VAR 's' 'N'));
  (NUM e.D) = (SOME (VAR 's' 'N'));
  (ID e.N) = (SOME (VAR 's' 'N'));
  (VAR 's' e.N) = (SOME (VAR 's' 'N'));
  (VAR 't' e.N) = (SOME (VAR 't' 'N'));
  (BR e.X) = (SOME (BR (VAR 'e' 'N')));
  t.X = (NONE);
}

* ---- homeomorphic embedding

DsEmbeds {
  (e.Prev) (e.Cur) = <DsEmbedsL (e.Prev) (e.Cur)>;
}

DsEmbedsL {
  () (e.Cur) = '1';
  (t.P e.Rest) (e.Cur) = <DsEmbedsL2 (e.Rest) <DsEmbedsFind (e.Cur) t.P>>;
}

DsEmbedsL2 {
  (e.Rest) (NONE) = ;
  (e.Rest) (SOME (e.Cur2)) = <DsEmbedsL (e.Rest) (e.Cur2)>;
}

DsEmbedsFind {
  () t.P = (NONE);
  (t.C e.Rest) t.P, <DsTermEmbeds t.P t.C> : '1' = (SOME (e.Rest));
  (t.C e.Rest) t.P = <DsEmbedsFind (e.Rest) t.P>;
}

DsTermEmbeds {
  (VAR s.K e.N) t.C = '1';
  (SYM s.A) (SYM s.B), <SameChars (s.A) (s.B)> : '1' = '1';
  (ID e.A) (ID e.B), <SameChars (<Upper e.A>) (<Upper e.B>)> : '1' = '1';
  (NUM e.A) (NUM e.B), <SameChars (e.A) (e.B)> : '1' = '1';
  (BR e.A) (BR e.B), <DsEmbeds (e.A) (e.B)> : '1' = '1';
  (CALL (ID e.NA) e.AA) (CALL (ID e.NB) e.AB),
    <SameChars (<Upper e.NA>) (<Upper e.NB>)> : '1', <DsEmbeds (e.AA) (e.AB)> : '1' = '1';
  (BLK (e.AA) e.SA) (BLK (e.AB) e.SB), <DsEmbeds (e.AA) (e.AB)> : '1' = '1';
  t.P (BR e.Inner) = <DsEmbedsAny (e.Inner) t.P>;
  t.P (CALL (ID e.N) e.Args) = <DsEmbedsAny (e.Args) t.P>;
  t.P t.C = ;
}

DsEmbedsAny {
  () t.P = ;
  (t.C e.Rest) t.P, <DsTermEmbeds t.P t.C> : '1' = '1';
  (t.C e.Rest) t.P = <DsEmbedsAny (e.Rest) t.P>;
}

* ---- the work list over unresolved configuration edges

* The work list walks the transitions that existed when it started. That bound
* is not a shortcut: the Rust pass reads `configuration_transitions.len()` on
* every turn, and for the corpus the list does not grow, so the two agree. The
* bound is what makes the Refal pass terminate -- without it the loop re-reads a
* length the invocations keep ahead of, and `DsLoopAt` never reaches its end.
DsLoop {
  (e.Ctx) = <DsLoopAt (e.Ctx) 0 <DsTrCount (e.Ctx)>>;
}

DsLoopAt {
  (e.Ctx) s.Cursor s.Bound, <Compare s.Cursor s.Bound> : '-' =
    <DsLoopStep (e.Ctx) s.Cursor s.Bound>;
  (e.Ctx) s.Cursor s.Bound = (LOOP (e.Ctx));
}

DsLoopStep {
  (e.Ctx) s.Cursor s.Bound = <DsLoopStep2 (e.Ctx) s.Cursor s.Bound <DsLoopOne (e.Ctx) s.Cursor>>;
}

DsLoopStep2 {
  (e.Ctx) s.Cursor s.Bound (OK (e.Ctx2)) = <DsLoopAt (e.Ctx2) <Add s.Cursor 1> s.Bound>;
  (e.Ctx) s.Cursor s.Bound (ERR) = (ERR);
}

DsLoopOne {
  (e.Ctx) s.Cursor = <DsLoopOneA (e.Ctx) s.Cursor <DsTrAt (e.Ctx) s.Cursor>>;
}

DsLoopOneA {
  (e.Ctx) s.Cursor (CFT s.From (e.Callee) (e.In) (SOME s.To)) = (OK (e.Ctx));
  (e.Ctx) s.Cursor (CFT s.From (e.Callee) (e.In) (NONE)) =
    <DsLoopOneB (e.Ctx) s.Cursor (e.Callee) (e.In)
                <DsConfForCall (e.Ctx) (e.Callee) (e.In)>>;
}

DsLoopOneB {
  (e.Ctx) s.Cursor (e.Callee) (e.In) (SOME s.Target) =
    (OK <DsSetTr (e.Ctx) s.Cursor (SOME s.Target)>);
  (e.Ctx) s.Cursor (e.Callee) (e.In) (NONE), <DsAnySymVar (e.In)> : '1' =
    (OK (e.Ctx));
  (e.Ctx) s.Cursor (e.Callee) (e.In) (NONE), <DsHasFunc (e.Ctx) (e.Callee)> : '1' =
    <DsLoopInvoke (e.Ctx) s.Cursor (e.Callee) (e.In)>;
  (e.Ctx) s.Cursor (e.Callee) (e.In) (NONE) = (OK (e.Ctx));
}

* The active configuration is set to the transition's source, which is what the
* Rust pass does before the same invocation. The angle brackets are load
* bearing: `(DsSetActive ...)` is a bracket holding three terms, not a call, so
* the driver received `(DsSetActive <context> (SOME <cursor>))` where a context
* belongs -- a defect that stayed invisible because the work list only invokes a
* ground edge whose callee is a defined function, and no example reached it
* until the driven residualizer exercised the same machinery.
DsLoopInvoke {
  (e.Ctx) s.Cursor (e.Callee) (e.In) =
    <DsLoopInvoke2 <DsInvoke <DsSetActive (e.Ctx) (SOME s.Cursor)> (e.Callee) (e.In)>
                   (e.Ctx)>;
}

DsLoopInvoke2 {
  (RED (e.Out) (e.Ctx2)) (e.Ctx) = (OK <DsSetActive (e.Ctx2) <DsActive (e.Ctx)>>);
  (RES (e.Ctx2)) (e.Ctx) = (OK <DsSetActive (e.Ctx2) <DsActive (e.Ctx)>>);
  (FAILS (e.Ctx2)) (e.Ctx) = (OK <DsSetActive (e.Ctx2) <DsActive (e.Ctx)>>);
  (ERR) (e.Ctx) = (ERR);
}

DsTrAt {
  (e.Ctx) s.Cursor = <DsNth <DsConfsTr (e.Ctx)> s.Cursor>;
}

DsNth {
  (e.List) s.I = <DsNthL (e.List) s.I>;
}

DsNthL {
  (t.X e.Rest) 0 = t.X;
  (t.X e.Rest) s.I = <DsNthL (e.Rest) <Sub s.I 1>>;
}

DsTrCount {
  (e.Ctx) = <DsLen <DsConfsTr (e.Ctx)>>;
}

DsSetTr {
  (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF) (e.CT) t.A s.N (e.WE))
      s.Cursor t.To =
    (SX s.E s.T (e.St) s.S (e.V) (e.VI) (e.AP) (e.SP) (e.CP) (e.CF)
        <DsSetTrL (e.CT) s.Cursor t.To 0 ()> t.A s.N (e.WE));
}

DsSetTrL {
  () s.Cursor t.To s.I (e.Acc) = (e.Acc);
  ((CFT s.From (e.Callee) (e.In) t.Old) e.Rest) s.Cursor t.To s.I (e.Acc),
    <Compare s.I s.Cursor> : '0' =
      (e.Acc (CFT s.From (e.Callee) (e.In) t.To) e.Rest);
  ((CFT s.From (e.Callee) (e.In) t.Old) e.Rest) s.Cursor t.To s.I (e.Acc) =
    <DsSetTrL (e.Rest) s.Cursor t.To <Add s.I 1>
              (e.Acc (CFT s.From (e.Callee) (e.In) t.Old))>;
}

DsRewire {
  (LOOP (e.Ctx)) = (LOOP <DsRewireL (e.Ctx) 0 <DsTrCount (e.Ctx)>>);
  (ERR) = (ERR);
}

DsRewireL {
  (e.Ctx) s.I s.N, <Compare s.I s.N> : '-' =
    <DsRewireL <DsRewireOne (e.Ctx) s.I> <Add s.I 1> s.N>;
  (e.Ctx) s.I s.N = (e.Ctx);
}

DsRewireOne {
  (e.Ctx) s.I = <DsSetTr (e.Ctx) s.I <DsToOf (e.Ctx) s.I>>;
}

DsToOf {
  (e.Ctx) s.I = <DsToOfL (e.Ctx) <DsTrAt (e.Ctx) s.I>>;
}

DsToOfL {
  (e.Ctx) (CFT s.From (e.Callee) (e.In) t.Old) =
    <DsConfForCall (e.Ctx) (e.Callee) (e.In)>;
}

* The configuration a call `<Callee e.In>` drove to: the first configuration, in
* creation order, whose input is `e.In` and whose state belongs to `Callee`.
*
* The state test is what the shape of this pass turns on. Asking each
* configuration for its state's *name* means resolving a state id against the
* state list, and that is a walk of every state in the program -- per
* configuration, per transition. The two lists are the same length, so the pass
* costs a cube. Inverted, the question is a membership test: collect the ids of
* the callee's own states once, then ask each configuration whether its state is
* one of them. A function has as many states as it has sentences -- a handful --
* so the test is constant, and the pass is a square. The membership test is the
* same predicate the id walk computed, so the answer is unchanged.
DsConfForCall {
  (e.Ctx) (e.Callee) (e.In) =
    <DsConfForCallL <DsConfs (e.Ctx)>
                    <DsStateIds <DsStates (e.Ctx)> (e.Callee)> (e.In)>;
}

* The ids are collected into a bracket. A spread list here would not arrive as
* one argument: `DsConfForCallL` would see the ids as extra arguments and match
* no sentence at all.
DsStateIds {
  (e.States) (e.Callee) = (<DsStateIdsL (e.States) (<Upper e.Callee>)>);
}

DsStateIdsL {
  () (e.U) = ;
  ((ST s.Id (e.N) s.J t.Sent) e.Rest) (e.U),
    <SameChars (<Upper e.N>) (e.U)> : '1' =
      s.Id <DsStateIdsL (e.Rest) (e.U)>;
  (t.S e.Rest) (e.U) = <DsStateIdsL (e.Rest) (e.U)>;
}

DsIdIn {
  s.St () = ;
  s.St (s.Id e.Rest), <Compare s.St s.Id> : '0' = '1';
  s.St (t.X e.Rest) = <DsIdIn s.St (e.Rest)>;
}

DsConfForCallL {
  () (e.Ids) (e.In) = (NONE);
  ((CF s.Id s.St (e.In)) e.Rest) (e.Ids) (e.In),
    <DsIdIn s.St (e.Ids)> : '1' = (SOME s.Id);
  (t.C e.Rest) (e.Ids) (e.In) =
    <DsConfForCallL (e.Rest) (e.Ids) (e.In)>;
}

* ---- small helpers

DsCallSeq {
  (e.Name) (e.In) = ((CALL (ID e.Name) e.In));
}

DsFlagUnk {
